= Mathematics
{wiki}

= Algebra
{parent=Mathematics}
{wiki}

= Number theory
{parent=Algebra}
{wiki}

= Primitive root
{parent=Number theory}
{wiki=Primitive_root_modulo_n}

A unit $g$ modulo $N$ is a primitive root when its multiplicative order is $\varphi(N)$.

= Quadratic residue
{parent=Number theory}
{wiki}

For an odd prime $p$, a nonzero class $a$ is a quadratic residue when $a\equiv x^2\pmod p$. These residues form an index-two subgroup of $(\mathbb Z/p\mathbb Z)^\times$.

= Primitive-root lifting criterion
{parent=Number theory}

If $g$ is primitive modulo an odd prime $p$ and $g^{p-1}\not\equiv1\pmod{p^2}$, then $g$ is primitive modulo every $p^k$.

= Möbius function
{parent=Number theory}
{wiki}

The Möbius function is $\mu(1)=1$, is zero on integers divisible by a prime square, and is $(-1)^r$ on products of $r$ distinct primes.

= Riemann zeta function
{c}
{parent=Number theory}
{wiki}

$\zeta(s)=\sum_{n\ge1}n^{-s}$ for $\Re s>1$; its Euler product is $\prod_p(1-p^{-s})^{-1}$.

= Jacobi symbol
{c}
{parent=Number theory}
{wiki}

For odd $n=\prod p_i^{e_i}$, $(a/n)=\prod(a/p_i)^{e_i}$. A value $1$ need not mean that $a$ is a square modulo composite $n$.

= Quadratic reciprocity
{parent=Number theory}
{wiki}

For distinct odd primes, $(p/q)(q/p)=(-1)^{(p-1)(q-1)/4}$.

= Carmichael number
{c}
{parent=Number theory}
{wiki}

A composite $N$ is Carmichael when $a^{N-1}\equiv1\pmod N$ for every $a$ coprime to $N$.

= Korselt criterion
{c}
{parent=Number theory}
{wiki=Korselt%27s_criterion}

A composite integer $N$ is Carmichael exactly when it is square-free and $p-1$ divides $N-1$ for every prime $p$ dividing $N$.

= Continued fraction
{parent=Number theory}
{wiki}

A simple continued fraction recursively expands a real number by taking integer parts and reciprocals. Its convergents are exceptionally good rational approximations.

= Liouville approximation theorem
{c}
{parent=Number theory}
{wiki=Liouville_number}

If an irrational algebraic number has degree $d$, then $|\alpha-p/q|>C(\alpha)q^{-d}$. Approximations of arbitrarily larger polynomial order therefore prove transcendence.

= Binary quadratic form
{parent=Number theory}
{wiki}

A binary quadratic form is $ax^2+bxy+cy^2$; integral changes of variables organize forms into equivalence classes.

= Euclid lemma
{parent=Number theory}
{c}
{wiki}

If a prime divides a product of integers, it divides at least one factor.

= Prime valuation
{parent=Number theory}
{c}
{wiki}

The prime valuation v_p(n) is the exponent of p in the prime factorization of n.

= Euler totient function
{parent=Number theory}
{c}
{wiki}

Euler’s totient function counts residue classes modulo n that are coprime to n.

= Euler theorem
{parent=Euler totient function}
{c}
{wiki}

If a is coprime to n, then a raised to the power phi(n) is congruent to one modulo n.

= Multiplicativity of the Euler totient function
{parent=Euler totient function}
{c}
{wiki}

For coprime positive integers m and n, the Chinese remainder theorem gives phi(mn)=phi(m)phi(n).

= Fermat little theorem
{parent=Number theory}
{c}
{wiki}

For a prime p, every integer a satisfies a^p congruent to a modulo p.

= Squarefree integer
{parent=Number theory}
{c}
{wiki}

A squarefree integer is not divisible by the square of any prime.

= Square factor
{parent=Number theory}
{c}
{wiki}

An integer has a square factor when some prime square divides it.

= Difference of two squares
{parent=Number theory}
{c}
{wiki}

The identity $r^2-s^2=(r-s)(r+s)$ converts a square difference into a factorization.

= Fermat factorization method
{parent=Difference of two squares}
{c}
{wiki=Fermat%27s_factorization_method}

Fermat factorization tests successive $r\geq\lceil\sqrt N\rceil$ until $r^2-N=s^2$, then returns $N=(r-s)(r+s)$.

= Rational number
{parent=Number theory}
{c}
{wiki}

A rational number is a quotient of two integers with nonzero denominator.

= Eventually periodic decimal
{parent=Rational number}
{c}
{wiki=Repeating_decimal}

A real number is rational exactly when its expansion in any integer base is eventually periodic.

= Algebraic number theory
{parent=Algebra}
{wiki}

= Ring of integers of a quadratic field
{parent=Algebraic number theory}
{wiki=Ring_of_integers}

For square-free $d\ne0,1$, an integral basis is $\{1,(1+\sqrt d)/2\}$ when $d\equiv1\pmod4$, and $\{1,\sqrt d\}$ otherwise. The discriminant is respectively $d$ and $4d$.

= Discriminant of a biquadratic field
{parent=Algebraic number theory}

The discriminant of a biquadratic field is the product of the fundamental discriminants of its three quadratic subfields.

= Algebraic integer module criterion
{parent=Algebraic number theory}
{c}
{wiki=Integral_element#Equivalent_definitions}

An element $x$ is integral over $\mathbb Z$ exactly when it acts stably on some faithful nonzero finitely generated $\mathbb Z$-module containing one.

= Ring generated by algebraic integers
{parent=Algebraic integer module criterion}
{c}

A ring generated over $\mathbb Z$ by finitely many algebraic integers is a finitely generated $\mathbb Z$-module.

= Integral dependence is transitive
{parent=Algebraic integer module criterion}
{c}
{wiki=Integral_element#Transitivity}

If $B$ is integral over $A$ and an element is integral over $B$, then it is integral over $A$.

= Integral basis
{parent=Algebraic number theory}
{c}
{wiki}

An integral basis is a $\mathbb Z$-basis of the ring of integers of a number field.

= Field discriminant
{parent=Integral basis}
{c}
{wiki}

The field discriminant is the discriminant of any integral basis and controls the index of every suborder.

= Field norm
{parent=Algebraic number theory}
{c}
{wiki}

The field norm $N_{L/K}(x)$ is the determinant of multiplication by $x$, equivalently the product of its conjugates when the extension is separable.

= Norm obstruction to being a square
{parent=Field norm}
{c}

If $x=y^2$ in a number field, then $N(x)=N(y)^2$; a norm that is not a square in the base field therefore obstructs square roots.

= Unit from norm one or minus one
{parent=Field norm}
{c}

An algebraic integer whose absolute norm is $1$ or $-1$ is a unit, because its conjugate product supplies an integral inverse.

= Field trace
{parent=Algebraic number theory}
{c}
{wiki}

The field trace $\operatorname{Tr}_{L/K}(x)$ is the trace of multiplication by $x$, equivalently the sum of its conjugates in a separable extension.

= Trace and norm integrality criterion for a quadratic extension
{parent=Field trace}
{c}

In a quadratic extension $L/K$, an element is integral exactly when its relative trace and norm lie in $\mathcal O_K$.

= Galois theory
{c}
{parent=Algebra}
{wiki}

= Galois group of a polynomial
{c}
{parent=Galois theory}
{wiki=Galois_group}

The Galois group of a separable polynomial acts faithfully on its roots; this action is transitive exactly when the polynomial is irreducible.

= Frobenius cycle type
{c}
{parent=Galois theory}

Over $\mathbb F_q$, Frobenius acts on roots of an irreducible degree-$n$ polynomial as an $n$-cycle. Factor degrees therefore give its cycle type.

= Dedekind factorization cycle test
{c}
{parent=Galois theory}

At an unramified prime, the degrees of the irreducible factors of a polynomial modulo that prime give the cycle type of an element of the Galois group.

= Cyclotomic polynomial
{parent=Galois theory}
{wiki}

$\Phi_n$ is the monic polynomial whose roots are the primitive $n$th roots of unity. It belongs to $\mathbb Z[X]$ and is irreducible over $\mathbb Q$.

= Artin fixed-field theorem
{c}
{parent=Galois theory}
{wiki=Artin%27s_theorem}

If a finite group $G$ acts faithfully by automorphisms on a field $L$, then $L/L^G$ is Galois, has degree $|G|$, and has Galois group $G$.

= Galois correspondence
{c}
{parent=Galois theory}
{wiki}

For a finite Galois extension, subgroups correspond in reverse order to intermediate fields by taking fixed fields and field-fixing subgroups.

= Polynomial discriminant
{parent=Galois theory}
{c}
{wiki=Discriminant#Polynomial_discriminant}

For a monic polynomial with roots $r_i$, the discriminant is $\prod_{i<j}(r_i-r_j)^2$ and vanishes exactly for a repeated root.

= Vandermonde determinant
{parent=Polynomial discriminant}
{c}
{wiki}

The Vandermonde product $\prod_{i<j}(r_i-r_j)$ is alternating, and its square is the discriminant of the monic polynomial with roots $r_i$.

= Discriminant of a depressed cubic
{parent=Polynomial discriminant}
{c}

The depressed cubic $x^3+ax+b$ has discriminant $-4a^3-27b^2$.

= Lagrange resolvent for a cubic
{parent=Galois theory}
{c}
{wiki=Lagrange_resolvent}

For cubic roots $\alpha_i$ and a cube root of unity $\omega$, the Fourier combinations $u=\alpha_1+\omega\alpha_2+\omega^2\alpha_3$ and $v$ have cubes satisfying a quadratic resolvent.

= Cubic resolvent
{parent=Lagrange resolvent for a cubic}
{c}

For $x^3+ax+b$, the Lagrange-resolvent cubes are roots of $X^2+27bX-27a^3$.

= Cardano formula
{parent=Cubic resolvent}
{c}
{wiki}

Cardano's formula solves a depressed cubic by taking square roots in its quadratic resolvent and then compatible cube roots.

= Solvability by radicals
{parent=Galois theory}
{c}
{wiki}

A polynomial is solvable by radicals when its roots lie in a tower obtained by adjoining successive roots; equivalently, its Galois group is solvable.

= Galois group of an irreducible cubic
{parent=Galois theory}
{c}

An irreducible cubic over a field of characteristic other than two has Galois group $A_3$ when its discriminant is a square and $S_3$ otherwise.

= Cubic discriminant criterion
{parent=Galois group of an irreducible cubic}
{c}

The discriminant square class detects whether the transitive cubic Galois group lies in the alternating group.

= Representation theory
{parent=Algebra}
{wiki}

= Character orthogonality
{parent=Representation theory}

Irreducible characters are orthonormal for $\langle\chi,\psi\rangle=|G|^{-1}\sum_g\chi(g)\overline{\psi(g)}$, and $\sum_\chi\chi(1)^2=|G|$.

= Permutation representation
{parent=Representation theory}
{wiki}

For a finite $G$-set $X$, the character of $\mathbb C[X]$ at $g$ is $|X^g|$. Its invariant dimension is the number of $G$-orbits.

= Schur lemma
{c}
{parent=Representation theory}
{wiki=Schur%27s_lemma}

An intertwiner between irreducible complex representations is either zero or an isomorphism; an endomorphism of one irreducible is scalar.

= Induced representation
{parent=Representation theory}
{wiki}

Induction extends a representation of a subgroup to the whole group; Frobenius reciprocity computes its multiplicities.

= Irreducible representation
{parent=Representation theory}
{c}
{wiki}

An irreducible representation has no nonzero proper invariant subspace.

= Eigenvalue orbit under conjugation
{parent=Irreducible representation}
{c}

If conjugation sends an operator to its inverse, the conjugating element sends its $\lambda$-eigenspace to the $\lambda^{-1}$-eigenspace.

= Infinite dihedral group
{parent=Representation theory}
{c}
{wiki}

The infinite dihedral group is $\langle r,t\mid t^2=1,\ trt^{-1}=r^{-1}\rangle$.

= Representation of the infinite dihedral group
{parent=Infinite dihedral group}
{c}

Its irreducible complex representations have dimension at most two; the two-dimensional family pairs eigenvalues $\lambda$ and $\lambda^{-1}$ of the rotation generator.

= Induced two-dimensional dihedral representation
{parent=Representation of the infinite dihedral group}
{c}

For $\lambda\ne\pm1$, the rotation acts by $\operatorname{diag}(\lambda,\lambda^{-1})$ and the reflection interchanges the two eigenlines.

= One-dimensional character
{parent=Representation theory}
{c}
{wiki=Character_theory}

A one-dimensional character is a homomorphism from a group to the multiplicative group of its scalar field.

= Finite quotient representation
{parent=Representation theory}
{c}

A representation factors through a finite quotient exactly when its kernel contains the defining kernel of that quotient.

= Indecomposable representation
{parent=Representation theory}
{c}
{wiki=Indecomposable_module}

An indecomposable representation cannot be expressed as a direct sum of two nonzero invariant subspaces.

= Nonsplit extension of representations
{parent=Indecomposable representation}
{c}

A nonsplit extension contains an invariant subrepresentation with no invariant complementary subspace.

= Unipotent representation
{parent=Indecomposable representation}
{c}

A unipotent operator has every eigenvalue equal to one and may create a nonsplit Jordan-block extension.

= Coding theory
{parent=Algebra}
{wiki}

= Linear code
{parent=Coding theory}
{wiki}

A binary linear $[n,k,d]$ code is a $k$-dimensional subspace of $\mathbb F_2^n$ with least nonzero weight $d$. A parity-check matrix presents it as a kernel.

= Parity extension
{parent=Coding theory}

Appending a parity bit makes every word even-weight, so an odd minimum distance $d$ becomes $d+1$ without changing code size.

= Hamming bound
{c}
{parent=Coding theory}
{wiki}

A binary length-$n$ code of size $M$ and distance at least $2t+1$ satisfies
$$
M\sum_{j=0}^t\binom nj\le2^n.
$$
Equality means the code is perfect.

= Rabin cryptosystem
{c}
{parent=Coding theory}
{wiki=Rabin_cryptosystem}

Rabin encryption sends $m$ to $m^2$ modulo a product of two secret primes. Decryption computes the four square roots by the Chinese remainder theorem.

= Prefix code
{parent=Coding theory}
{c}
{wiki=Prefix_code}

In a prefix code no codeword is the prefix of another, so concatenated messages have unique instantaneous decoding.

= Kraft inequality
{parent=Prefix code}
{c}
{wiki=Kraft–McMillan_inequality}

Binary prefix-code lengths satisfy $\sum_i2^{-l_i}\leq1$; conversely, any positive integer lengths satisfying this bound can be realized by a prefix code.

= Shannon-Fano coding
{parent=Prefix code}
{c}
{wiki=Shannon–Fano_coding}

Shannon--Fano coding assigns a symbol of probability $p_i$ a prefix-code length near $-\log_2p_i$.

= Entropy lower bound for prefix codes
{parent=Prefix code}
{c}

Every binary prefix code has expected length at least its source entropy; Shannon lengths achieve expected length strictly below $H+1$.

= Linear algebra
{parent=Algebra}
{wiki}

= Householder reflection
{c}
{parent=Linear algebra}
{wiki=Householder_transformation}

For unit $v$, $I-2vv^T$ is an orthogonal reflection. Successive Householder similarities reduce a symmetric matrix while preserving symmetry.

= Change of basis
{parent=Linear algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

= Circulant matrix
{parent=Linear algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

= Invertible matrix
{parent=Linear algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

= Kernel of a linear map
{parent=Linear algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

= Matrix determinant lemma
{parent=Linear algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

= Matrix inverse
{parent=Linear algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

= Similar matrix
{parent=Linear algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

= Transpose similarity
{parent=Similar matrix}
{c}
{wiki}

Every square matrix over a field is similar to its transpose.

= Linear operator theory
{parent=Linear algebra}
{wiki=Operator_theory}

Linear operator theory studies linear maps, their spectra, invariant subspaces, and normal forms.

= Real spectral theorem
{parent=Linear operator theory}
{wiki=Spectral_theorem}

Every real symmetric matrix is orthogonally diagonalizable. Distinct eigenspaces are orthogonal because symmetry gives $\lambda\langle u,v\rangle=\mu\langle u,v\rangle$.

= Power method
{parent=Linear operator theory}
{wiki=Power_iteration}

Iterate $q^{(k+1)}=Aq^{(k)}/\|Aq^{(k)}\|$. With a unique dominant eigenvalue and a nonzero initial dominant component, directions converge at the spectral-ratio rate.

= Characteristic polynomial
{parent=Linear operator theory}
{c}
{wiki}

= Eigenvalue interlacing
{parent=Linear operator theory}
{c}
{wiki}

Eigenvalue interlacing bounds the ordered eigenvalues of a compressed or rank-modified operator between those of the original operator.

= Jordan normal form
{parent=Linear operator theory}
{c}
{wiki}

= Jordan block
{parent=Jordan normal form}
{c}
{wiki}

A Jordan block has one eigenvalue on its diagonal and ones on its superdiagonal.

= Generalized eigenvector
{parent=Jordan normal form}
{c}
{wiki}

A generalized eigenvector lies in the kernel of a positive power of A minus lambda I.

= Matrix exponential
{parent=Linear operator theory}
{c}
{wiki}

= Minimal polynomial
{parent=Linear operator theory}
{c}
{wiki}

= Nilpotent linear map
{parent=Linear operator theory}
{c}
{wiki}

= Eigenvalue
{parent=Linear operator theory}
{c}
{wiki}

A scalar $\lambda$ is an eigenvalue of $A$ when $Av=\lambda v$ for some nonzero vector $v$.

= Eigenspace
{parent=Eigenvalue}
{c}
{wiki}

The eigenspace for $\lambda$ is $\ker(A-\lambda I)$.

= Diagonalizable matrix
{parent=Eigenvalue}
{c}
{wiki=Diagonalizable_matrix}

A matrix is diagonalizable exactly when its eigenvectors span the whole vector space.

= Spectral decomposition
{parent=Diagonalizable matrix}
{c}
{wiki}

A spectral decomposition expresses an operator through its eigenvalues and projections onto its eigenspaces.

= Unilateral shift operator
{parent=Linear operator theory}
{c}
{wiki=Shift_operator}

The unilateral shifts on sequences move every component one place left or right. Their spectra illustrate behavior absent in finite dimensions.

= Point spectrum
{parent=Unilateral shift operator}
{c}
{wiki=Spectrum_(functional_analysis)#Point_spectrum}

The point spectrum of an operator is its set of eigenvalues.

= Rayleigh quotient
{parent=Linear operator theory}
{c}
{wiki}

For a symmetric matrix, the Rayleigh quotient is $x^TAx/(x^Tx)$.

= Rayleigh-Ritz variational principle
{parent=Rayleigh quotient}
{c}
{wiki=Rayleigh_quotient#Bounds_for_Hermitian_matrices}

The maximum and minimum Rayleigh quotients of a real symmetric matrix are its largest and smallest eigenvalues.

= Cyclic vector
{parent=Linear operator theory}
{c}
{wiki}

A cyclic vector has iterates under an operator that span the whole vector space.

= Companion matrix
{parent=Linear operator theory}
{c}
{wiki}

A companion matrix represents multiplication by x in the power basis modulo a monic polynomial.

= Invariant direct-sum decomposition
{parent=Linear operator theory}
{c}
{wiki}

An invariant direct-sum decomposition splits a space into nonzero subspaces preserved by the operator.

= Rational canonical form
{parent=Linear operator theory}
{c}
{wiki}

Rational canonical form expresses a linear map as a direct sum of companion matrices of invariant factors, and works over any field.

= Multilinear algebra
{parent=Linear algebra}
{wiki}

Multilinear algebra studies maps linear in each of several arguments, including determinants and tensor products.

= Tensor
{parent=Multilinear algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

= Einstein notation
{parent=Tensor}
{c}
{wiki}

Einstein notation sums automatically over an index repeated once up and once down, or twice in Euclidean coordinates.

= Tensor contraction
{parent=Multilinear algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

= Reciprocal basis
{parent=Multilinear algebra}
{c}
{wiki=Reciprocal_lattice#Reciprocal_basis}

For a basis $e_1,e_2,e_3$ with scalar triple product $\Delta$, the reciprocal vectors are cyclic cross products divided by $\Delta$ and satisfy $f_i\cdot e_j=\delta_{ij}$.

= Scalar triple product
{parent=Multilinear algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

The scalar triple product $a\cdot(b\times c)$ is the signed volume of the parallelepiped spanned by three vectors.

= Oriented volume
{parent=Scalar triple product}
{c}
{wiki=Orientation_(vector_space)}

An oriented volume retains the sign of a determinant; reversing the orientation changes that sign.

= Determinant
{parent=Multilinear algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

The determinant is the alternating multilinear volume scale of a square matrix; it is nonzero exactly for invertible matrices.

= Tridiagonal determinant recurrence
{parent=Determinant}
{c}

Expanding a tridiagonal determinant at an endpoint produces a second-order recurrence involving the two smaller leading principal determinants.

= Linear system
{parent=Linear algebra}
{c}
{wiki=System_of_linear_equations}

A linear system $Ax=b$ is consistent when $b$ lies in the column space of $A$; its solutions form a translate of $\ker A$.

= Fredholm alternative for a matrix
{parent=Linear system}
{c}

The system $Ax=b$ is solvable exactly when $b$ is orthogonal to every vector in $\ker A^T$.

= Symmetric and antisymmetric parts of a matrix
{parent=Linear algebra}
{c}

Every square matrix decomposes uniquely as $A=(A+A^T)/2+(A-A^T)/2$, a symmetric part plus an antisymmetric part.

= Axis-angle decomposition
{parent=Linear algebra}
{c}
{wiki=Axis–angle_representation}

An axis-angle map fixes or scales one axis and acts on its perpendicular plane by a rotation, possibly combined with a dilation.

= Inverse of an axis-angle linear map
{parent=Axis-angle decomposition}
{c}

Invert the axial scalar and invert the perpendicular complex scalar $\alpha+i\gamma$ as $(\alpha-i\gamma)/(\alpha^2+\gamma^2)$.

= Reflection matrix
{parent=Linear algebra}
{c}
{wiki=Reflection_(mathematics)}

A plane reflection with unit normal $n$ is $I-2nn^T$; it fixes the plane $n^\perp$ and negates $n$.

= Rotation matrix
{parent=Linear algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

A rotation matrix is an orthogonal matrix with determinant $1$.

= Matrix geometric series
{parent=Linear algebra}
{c}

For invertible $I-A$, $\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}A^j=(I-A^n)(I-A)^{-1}$.

= Adjugate matrix
{parent=Linear algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

The adjugate is the transpose of the cofactor matrix and satisfies $A\operatorname{adj}(A)=(\det A)I$.

= Matrix equivalence
{parent=Linear algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

Equivalent matrices represent the same linear map after independent changes of domain and codomain bases.

= Rank inequality for a composition
{parent=Linear algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

For maps through a finite-dimensional intermediate space V, rank(alpha beta) is at least rank(alpha)+rank(beta)-dim(V).

= Schur complement
{parent=Linear algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

For an invertible block P, the Schur complement of P in a block matrix is $S-RP^{-1}Q$.

= Orthogonal similarity
{parent=Linear algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

Orthogonal similarity maps A to QAQ^T and preserves eigenvalues and symmetry.

= Product of symmetric matrices
{parent=Linear algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

Every complex square matrix is a product of two symmetric matrices, one invertible.

= Inner product
{parent=Linear algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

= Quadratic form
{parent=Linear algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

A quadratic form is a homogeneous degree-two function $Q(v)$ represented in coordinates by $v^TAv$ with $A$ symmetric.

= Polarization identity
{parent=Quadratic form}
{c}
{wiki}

Over a field of characteristic other than two, a quadratic form recovers its symmetric bilinear form by
$$B(u,v)=\frac12\bigl(Q(u+v)-Q(u)-Q(v)\bigr).$$

= Positive semidefinite bilinear form
{parent=Quadratic form}
{c}
{wiki=Definite_matrix}

A symmetric bilinear form is positive semidefinite when $B(v,v)\geq0$ for every $v$, and positive definite when equality is possible only for $v=0$.

= Sylvester's law of inertia
{parent=Quadratic form}
{c}
{wiki=Sylvester%27s_law_of_inertia}

Every real quadratic form is congruent to a diagonal form with entries $+1,-1,0$, and the numbers of entries of each kind are invariant.

= Totally isotropic subspace
{parent=Quadratic form}
{c}
{wiki=Isotropic_quadratic_form}

A subspace $E$ is totally isotropic for $B$ when $B(u,v)=0$ for all $u,v\in E$. For a nondegenerate real form of inertia $(p,q)$, its dimension is at most $\min(p,q)$.

= Quadratic form gradient
{parent=Quadratic form}
{c}

For symmetric $A$, the quadratic form $x^TAx$ has gradient $2Ax$.

= Schur product theorem
{parent=Linear algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

The entrywise product of two positive semidefinite matrices is positive semidefinite. Writing each matrix as a sum of rank-one Gram matrices reduces the claim to $(uu^T)\circ(vv^T)=(u\circ v)(u\circ v)^T$.

= Dual vector space
{parent=Linear algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

The dual $V^*$ consists of the linear maps from $V$ to its scalar field.

= Dual basis
{parent=Dual vector space}
{c}
{wiki}

For a basis $(v_i)$, its dual basis $(v_i^*)$ is characterized by $v_i^*(v_j)=\delta_{ij}$.

= Polynomial moment functional
{parent=Dual vector space}
{c}

A polynomial moment functional evaluates a polynomial through a weighted integral, such as $p\mapsto\int p(t)w(t)dt$; finitely many moments determine it on a bounded-degree polynomial space.

= Orthogonal group
{parent=Linear algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

The real orthogonal group is $O(n)=\{R:R^TR=I\}$. Its tangent space at the identity is the vector space of skew-symmetric matrices.

= Skew-symmetric matrix
{parent=Orthogonal group}
{c}
{wiki}

A matrix is skew-symmetric when $A^T=-A$.

= Tensor product of linear maps
{parent=Linear algebra}
{c}
{wiki=Tensor_product#Tensor_product_of_linear_maps}

The tensor product is determined by $(A\otimes B)(x\otimes y)=Ax\otimes By$ and linear extension.

= Algebraic number
{parent=Algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

= Countability of algebraic numbers
{parent=Algebraic number}
{c}

Integer polynomials form a countable set and each has finitely many roots, so the algebraic numbers are countable.

= Bezout identity
{parent=Algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

= Countable set
{parent=Algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

= Countable union of countable sets
{parent=Countable set}
{c}
{wiki}

A countable union of countable sets is countable, using a diagonal enumeration.

= Eigenfunction expansion
{parent=Algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

= Field extension
{parent=Algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

= Finite field
{parent=Algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

= Function composition
{parent=Algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

= Injective function
{parent=Algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

= Irrational number
{parent=Algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

= Irrotational vector field
{parent=Algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

= Isotropic tensor
{parent=Algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

= Linear recurrence relation
{parent=Algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

= Affine recurrence
{parent=Linear recurrence relation}
{c}

An affine recurrence has the form $x_{n+1}=Ax_n+b$ and is solved by a matrix geometric sum.

= Stable affine recurrence
{parent=Affine recurrence}
{c}

If the spectral radius of $A$ is below one, $x_{n+1}=Ax_n+b$ converges to the unique fixed point $(I-A)^{-1}b$.

= Linearization of a dynamical system
{parent=Algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

= Mathematical induction
{parent=Algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

= Strong induction
{parent=Mathematical induction}
{c}
{wiki}

Strong induction allows the step for an integer to use the result for every smaller integer.

= Minimal number of generators
{parent=Algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

= Nilpotent polynomial
{parent=Algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

= Noetherian ring
{parent=Algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

= Hilbert basis theorem
{parent=Noetherian ring}
{c}
{wiki}

A polynomial ring over a Noetherian ring is Noetherian.

= P-adic valuation
{parent=Algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

= Pauli matrix
{parent=Algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

= Pigeonhole principle
{parent=Algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

= Polynomial interpolation error
{parent=Algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

= Hermite interpolation
{parent=Polynomial interpolation error}
{c}
{wiki}

Hermite interpolation prescribes values and finitely many derivatives of a polynomial at distinct points.

= RSA cryptosystem
{parent=Algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

= Root of unity
{parent=Algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

= Smith normal form
{parent=Algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

= Star-shaped set
{parent=Algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

= Surjective function
{parent=Algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

= Transcendental number
{parent=Algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

= Liouville number
{parent=Transcendental number}
{c}
{wiki}

A Liouville number admits rational approximations of every fixed polynomial order; Liouville's approximation theorem makes every such irrational number transcendental.

= Unique factorization domain
{parent=Algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

= Unit in a ring
{parent=Algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

= Wilson theorem
{parent=Algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

= Fundamental theorem of algebra
{parent=Algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

Every nonconstant complex polynomial has a complex root and therefore factors completely into linear factors over $\mathbb C$.

= Group theory
{parent=Algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

Group theory studies groups, homomorphisms, actions, quotients, and symmetry.

= Center of a group
{parent=Group theory}
{c}
{wiki}

= Conjugate subgroup
{parent=Group theory}
{c}
{wiki}

= Group action
{parent=Group theory}
{c}
{wiki}

= Faithful group action
{parent=Group action}
{c}
{wiki}

A group action is faithful when only the identity acts as the identity on every point.

= Orbit of a group action
{parent=Group action}
{c}
{wiki}

The orbit of $x$ is $Gx=\{gx:g\in G\}$.

= Stabilizer subgroup
{parent=Group action}
{c}
{wiki}

The stabilizer of $x$ is the subgroup $\{g:gx=x\}$.

= Symmetric group action on subsets
{parent=Group action}
{c}
{wiki}

The symmetric group acts on $k$-element subsets by applying a permutation to every member.

= Coset action
{parent=Group action}
{c}
{wiki}

A group acts on the left cosets of a subgroup by left multiplication; its kernel is the core of that subgroup.

= Order of a group element
{parent=Group theory}
{c}
{wiki}

= Quotient group
{parent=Group theory}
{c}
{wiki}

= Special linear group
{parent=Group theory}
{c}
{wiki}

= Group homomorphism
{parent=Group theory}
{c}
{wiki}

A group homomorphism preserves multiplication: $\phi(xy)=\phi(x)\phi(y)$.

= Homomorphism between cyclic groups
{parent=Group homomorphism}
{c}
{wiki}

A homomorphism from $C_n$ is determined by the image of one generator; a surjection $C_n\to C_m$ exists exactly when $m\mid n$.

= Kernel of a group homomorphism
{parent=Group homomorphism}
{c}
{wiki}

The kernel $\{g:\phi(g)=1\}$ is a normal subgroup of the domain.

= Finite group theory
{parent=Group theory}
{c}
{wiki}

Finite group theory studies groups with finitely many elements.

= Alternating group
{parent=Finite group theory}
{c}
{wiki}

= P-group
{parent=Finite group theory}
{c}
{wiki}

= Symmetric group
{parent=Finite group theory}
{c}
{wiki}

= Quaternion group
{parent=Finite group theory}
{c}
{wiki}

The quaternion group $Q_8=\{\pm1,\pm i,\pm j,\pm k\}$ is a nonabelian Dedekind group.

= Simple group
{parent=Finite group theory}
{c}
{wiki}

A nontrivial simple group has no normal subgroups other than the identity subgroup and itself.

= Smallest nonabelian simple group
{parent=Simple group}
{c}
{wiki}

Every finite nonabelian simple group has order at least 60; the alternating group A5 attains this bound.

= General linear group over a finite field
{parent=Finite group theory}
{c}
{wiki}

The group $GL_n(\mathbb F_q)$ consists of invertible $n$ by $n$ matrices over the finite field $\mathbb F_q$.

= Special linear group over a finite field
{parent=General linear group over a finite field}
{c}
{wiki}

The determinant-one matrices form the normal subgroup $SL_n(\mathbb F_q)=\ker(\det)$.

= Dihedral group
{parent=Finite group theory}
{c}
{wiki}

The dihedral group $D_{2n}$ is generated by a rotation $r$ of order $n$ and a reflection $s$ with $srs=r^{-1}$; it has order $2n$.

= Sylow theorem
{parent=Finite group theory}
{c}
{wiki}

Sylow’s theorems control the existence, conjugacy, and number of maximal p-subgroups of a finite group.

= Conjugation action on Sylow subgroups
{parent=Sylow theorem}
{c}
{wiki}

A group, or one of its Sylow subgroups, acts on the set of Sylow subgroups by conjugation.

= Cauchy theorem for groups
{parent=Finite group theory}
{c}
{wiki}

If a prime divides the order of a finite group, the group contains an element of that prime order.

= Composition series
{parent=Finite group theory}
{c}
{wiki}

A composition series is a finite subnormal chain whose successive quotients are simple.

= Klein four-group
{parent=Finite group theory}
{c}
{wiki}

The Klein four-group is the abelian group with three nonidentity elements, all of order two.

= Torsion element
{parent=Group theory}
{c}
{wiki}

A torsion element has finite order.

= Free abelian group
{parent=Group theory}
{c}
{wiki}

A free abelian group has a basis over $\mathbb Z$ and is isomorphic to a direct sum of copies of $\mathbb Z$.

= Normal subgroup
{parent=Group theory}
{c}
{wiki}

A subgroup $N\le G$ is normal when $gNg^{-1}=N$ for every $g\in G$.

= Preimage of a normal subgroup
{parent=Normal subgroup}
{c}
{wiki}

The preimage of a normal subgroup under any group homomorphism is normal.

= Image of a normal subgroup
{parent=Normal subgroup}
{c}
{wiki}

Under a surjective homomorphism, the image of a normal subgroup is normal.

= Dedekind group
{parent=Group theory}
{c}
{wiki}

A Dedekind group is a group in which every subgroup is normal.

= Projective linear group
{parent=Group theory}
{c}
{wiki}

The projective linear group is $PGL_n(F)=GL_n(F)/F^\times$ and acts on projective space.

= Mobius transformation
{parent=Projective linear group}
{c}
{wiki}

= Fixed point of a Mobius transformation
{parent=Mobius transformation}
{c}
{wiki}

A fixed point of a Möbius transformation satisfies a quadratic equation on the Riemann sphere.

= Classification of Mobius transformations by trace
{parent=Mobius transformation}
{c}
{wiki}

For determinant-one real representatives, trace magnitude below, equal to, or above two gives elliptic, parabolic, or hyperbolic type.

= Matrix representative of a Mobius transformation
{parent=Projective linear group}
{c}
{wiki}

A Möbius transformation is represented by an invertible two-by-two matrix, with nonzero scalar multiples representing the same map.

= Semidirect product
{parent=Group theory}
{c}
{wiki}

A semidirect product combines a normal subgroup with a subgroup acting on it by automorphisms.

= Affine group over a finite field
{parent=Semidirect product}
{c}
{wiki}

The one-dimensional affine group over $\mathbb F_q$ consists of maps $x\mapsto ax+b$ with $a
e0$.

= Solvable group
{parent=Group theory}
{c}
{wiki}

A solvable group has a subnormal series whose factor groups are abelian.

= Homogeneous polynomial
{parent=Algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

A polynomial is homogeneous of degree $n$ when scaling all variables by $\lambda$ scales its value by $\lambda^n$.

= Commutative algebra
{parent=Algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

Commutative algebra studies commutative rings, ideals, modules, and their spectra.

= Integral domain
{parent=Commutative algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

An integral domain is a nonzero commutative ring with identity and without zero divisors.

= Principal ideal domain
{parent=Integral domain}
{c}
{wiki}

A principal ideal domain is an integral domain whose every ideal has one generator.

= Euclidean domain
{parent=Principal ideal domain}
{c}
{wiki}

A Euclidean domain admits division with remainder measured by a decreasing Euclidean function.

= Fiber product of rings
{parent=Commutative algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

A fiber product of rings consists of pairs having the same image in a third ring.

= Unit modulo a prime power
{parent=Commutative algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

Modulo a prime power, a class is a unit exactly when it is not divisible by that prime.

= Nilpotent element modulo a prime power
{parent=Commutative algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

Modulo p^e, a class is nilpotent exactly when its reduction modulo p is zero.

= Reduction map on unit groups
{parent=Commutative algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

Reduction between quotient rings sends units to units and is surjective when every target unit avoids the same prime factors.

= Polynomial content
{parent=Commutative algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

The content of a polynomial is a greatest common divisor of its coefficients, up to a unit.

= Primitive polynomial
{parent=Polynomial content}
{c}
{wiki}

A primitive polynomial has unit content.

= Gauss lemma for polynomials
{parent=Primitive polynomial}
{c}
{wiki}

Over a UFD, products of primitive polynomials are primitive and factorization over the fraction field descends after content is removed.

= Eisenstein criterion
{parent=Commutative algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

Eisenstein’s criterion proves irreducibility when one prime divides every nonleading coefficient but its square misses the constant term.

= Module theory
{parent=Commutative algebra}
{c}
{wiki=Module_(mathematics)}

Module theory extends linear algebra by allowing scalars from a ring rather than only a field.

= Cyclic module
{parent=Module theory}
{c}
{wiki}

A module is cyclic when one element $m$ generates it: $M=Rm$.

= Irreducible module
{parent=Module theory}
{c}
{wiki=Simple_module}

An irreducible, or simple, module is nonzero and has no submodules other than zero and itself.

= Simple module over a commutative ring
{parent=Irreducible module}
{c}

Every simple module over a commutative ring $R$ is isomorphic to $R/\mathfrak m$ for a maximal ideal $\mathfrak m$; its annihilator is therefore maximal.

= Annihilator of a module
{parent=Module theory}
{c}
{wiki=Annihilator_(ring_theory)}

The annihilator $\operatorname{Ann}_R(M)$ is the ideal of scalars that kill every element of $M$.

= Structure theorem for finitely generated modules over a principal ideal domain
{parent=Module theory}
{c}
{wiki=Structure_theorem_for_finitely_generated_modules_over_a_principal_ideal_domain}

Every finitely generated module over a PID is a direct sum of a free module and cyclic prime-power torsion modules.

= Elementary divisor
{parent=Structure theorem for finitely generated modules over a principal ideal domain}
{c}
{wiki}

An elementary divisor is a prime power $p^e$ occurring in the cyclic decomposition of a finitely generated torsion module.

= Prime ideal
{parent=Commutative algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

An ideal $\mathfrak p$ is prime when $ab\in\mathfrak p$ implies $a\in\mathfrak p$ or $b\in\mathfrak p$, equivalently when the quotient is an integral domain.

= Irreducible real polynomial
{parent=Commutative algebra}
{c}

The irreducible polynomials over $\mathbb R$ have degree one, or degree two with negative discriminant.

= Kummer irreducibility criterion
{parent=Commutative algebra}
{c}

Over a field containing the relevant roots of unity, $X^n-a$ is irreducible when the valuation data prevent $a$ from being a proper prime-divisor power, with the standard fourth-power exception.

= Foundations of mathematics
{parent=Mathematics}
{wiki}

= Set theory
{parent=Foundations of mathematics}
{wiki}

= Axiom of choice
{parent=Set theory}
{wiki}

Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function. In ZF this is equivalent to the well-ordering theorem.

= Hartogs theorem
{c}
{parent=Set theory}
{wiki=Hartogs_number}

For every set $X$ there is a least ordinal $h(X)$ that does not inject into $X$.

= Tarski cardinal-square theorem
{c}
{parent=Set theory}

In ZF, if $X\times X\cong X$ for every infinite $X$, apply this to $X\sqcup h(X)$. Tarski's comparison argument forces $X$ to inject into the ordinal $h(X)$, so $X$ is well-orderable. The converse is the well-orderable-cardinal identity $\kappa^2=\kappa$.

= Image and preimage of a function
{parent=Set theory}
{c}
{wiki}

Images send subsets of a domain forward through a function, while preimages pull subsets of the codomain back.

= Fiber product of sets
{parent=Set theory}
{c}
{wiki}

The fiber product of maps f:A to B and g:A-prime to B consists of pairs with equal images in B.

= Cantor diagonal argument
{parent=Set theory}
{c}
{wiki}

Cantor’s diagonal argument constructs an object differing from the nth listed object in its nth coordinate, proving no proposed enumeration is complete.

= Ordinal
{parent=Set theory}
{c}
{wiki}

An ordinal is a transitive set well-ordered by membership and represents the order type of a well-order.

= Order type
{parent=Ordinal}
{c}
{wiki}

The order type of a well-order is the unique ordinal isomorphic to it.

= Mostowski collapse theorem
{parent=Order type}
{c}
{wiki}

Every well-founded extensional relation is uniquely isomorphic to membership on a transitive set.

= Transfinite recursion
{parent=Ordinal}
{c}
{wiki}

Transfinite recursion defines a value at each ordinal from the function of all earlier values.

= Ordinal addition
{parent=Ordinal}
{c}
{wiki}

Ordinal addition is ordered concatenation and is associative but generally not commutative.

= Ordinal interval
{parent=Ordinal}
{c}

For $\alpha<\beta$, the ordinal interval $\beta\setminus\alpha$ is the ordered tail from $\alpha$ inclusive to $\beta$ exclusive.

= First uncountable ordinal
{parent=Ordinal}
{c}
{wiki}

The first uncountable ordinal $\omega_1$ is the set of all countable ordinals.

= Tail of the first uncountable ordinal
{parent=First uncountable ordinal}
{c}

Deleting any countable initial segment from $\omega_1$ leaves a well-order of type $\omega_1$.

= Cantor normal form
{parent=Ordinal}
{c}
{wiki}

Every nonzero ordinal has a unique finite expression $\omega^{\delta_1}n_1+\cdots+\omega^{\delta_r}n_r$ with decreasing exponents and positive finite coefficients.

= Leading term of an ordinal
{parent=Cantor normal form}
{c}

The leading term $\omega^\delta n$ is the highest-exponent term in Cantor normal form; the remaining tail is strictly below $\omega^\delta$.

= Indecomposable ordinal
{parent=Ordinal}
{c}
{wiki=Indecomposable_ordinal}

The additively indecomposable ordinals are exactly the powers $\omega^\delta$.

= Hessenberg natural sum
{parent=Ordinal}
{c}
{wiki=Natural_operations}

The Hessenberg natural sum aligns equal exponents in two Cantor normal forms and adds their finite coefficients; unlike ordinal addition it is commutative.

= Shuffle bound for ordinal partitions
{parent=Hessenberg natural sum}
{c}

If a well-order is the union of suborders of types $\xi$ and $\eta$, its type is at most $\xi\mathbin\#\eta$.

= Ordinal partition bound
{parent=Shuffle bound for ordinal partitions}
{c}

If an ordinal is partitioned into two copies of type $\beta$, its type is below $\beta+\beta+\beta$; two copies do not always give a strict bound.

= Computability theory
{parent=Foundations of mathematics}
{wiki}

= Primitive recursive function
{parent=Computability theory}
{wiki}

Primitive recursive functions are generated by zero, successor, and projections under composition and primitive recursion.

= Diagonal argument
{parent=Computability theory}
{wiki}

A list $f(n,k)$ of all functions in a class closed under diagonal substitution and successor misses $d(k)=f(k,k)+1$.

= Recursively enumerable set
{parent=Computability theory}
{wiki}

A set is recursively enumerable when some algorithm lists its elements, equivalently when it is the halting domain of a partial computable function.

= Recursion theorem
{c}
{parent=Computability theory}
{wiki=Kleene%27s_recursion_theorem}

Kleene recursion gives programs access to their own effective descriptions: every computable transformation of program indices has a semantic fixed point.

= Many-one reduction
{parent=Computability theory}
{c}
{wiki=Many-one_reduction}

A many-one reduction $A\leq_mB$ is a total computable $f$ satisfying $x\in A$ exactly when $f(x)\in B$.

= Many-one degree
{parent=Many-one reduction}
{c}
{wiki=Many-one_reduction#Many-one_equivalence}

Two sets have the same many-one degree when each many-one reduces to the other.

= Computable reduction by staged enumeration
{parent=Many-one reduction}
{c}

A staged-enumeration reduction controls an output language by enumerating more elements at each simulation stage and changing behavior when a source computation halts.

= Index set
{parent=Computability theory}
{c}
{wiki=Index_set_(recursion_theory)}

An index set is a set of program codes whose membership depends only on the partial function or enumerable language computed, rather than the code's syntax.

= Extensional property of programs
{parent=Index set}
{c}

An extensional program property gives identical answers to all codes computing the same object.

= Rice theorem
{parent=Index set}
{c}
{wiki=Rice%27s_theorem}

Every nontrivial extensional property of partial computable functions or computably enumerable languages is undecidable.

= Cofinite index set
{parent=Index set}
{c}

The cofinite index set contains codes whose enumerable language has finite complement.

= Padding lemma
{parent=Computability theory}
{c}
{wiki=Padding_lemma_(computability_theory)}

The padding lemma produces infinitely many syntactically distinct program indices for the same computed function or language.

= Dovetailing
{parent=Computability theory}
{c}
{wiki=Dovetailing_(computer_science)}

Dovetailing interleaves finite stages of multiple computations so that every computation that halts is eventually observed.

= S-m-n theorem
{parent=Computability theory}
{c}
{wiki=Smn_theorem}

The S-m-n theorem compiles fixed parameters into program codes by a total computable transformation.

= Formal language theory
{parent=Foundations of mathematics}
{wiki=Formal_language}

= Formal grammar
{parent=Formal language theory}
{wiki}

A grammar $(N,\Sigma,P,S)$ generates the terminal words reachable from $S$ by finitely many productions.

= Context-free language
{parent=Formal language theory}
{wiki}

A grammar is context-free when each production has one nonterminal on its left.

= Pumping lemma for context-free languages
{parent=Formal language theory}
{wiki}

For some $p$, every sufficiently long $w$ in a context-free language has $w=uvxyz$ with $|vxy|\le p$, $|vy|>0$, and $uv^ixy^iz$ in the language for all $i\ge0$.

= Deterministic finite automaton
{parent=Formal language theory}
{wiki}

A deterministic finite automaton has finitely many states and one transition for each state-symbol pair.

= Regular language
{parent=Formal language theory}
{wiki}

A language is regular exactly when it is accepted by a finite automaton, or equivalently has finitely many Myhill-Nerode classes.

= Pumping lemma for regular languages
{parent=Formal language theory}
{wiki}

Every sufficiently long word in a regular language has a nonempty early segment that may be repeated any number of times while remaining in the language.

= Myhill-Nerode theorem
{c}
{parent=Formal language theory}
{wiki=Myhill–Nerode_theorem}

A language is regular exactly when indistinguishability by all suffixes has finitely many equivalence classes; these classes form its minimal DFA.

= Closure properties of formal languages
{parent=Formal language theory}
{c}
{wiki=Abstract_family_of_languages}

A language class is closed under an operation when applying that operation to members always produces another member of the class.

= Computably enumerable language
{parent=Formal language theory}
{c}
{wiki=Recursively_enumerable_language}

A language is computably enumerable when a machine recognizes its members, possibly running forever on nonmembers.

= Halting problem
{parent=Computably enumerable language}
{c}
{wiki}

The halting set is computably enumerable but undecidable, and its complement is not computably enumerable.

= Diagonal halting set
{parent=Halting problem}
{c}

For an effective enumeration $W_w$, the diagonal halting set is $K=\{w:w\in W_w\}$.

= Many-one completeness of the halting problem
{parent=Diagonal halting set}
{c}

Every computably enumerable set many-one reduces to the diagonal halting set.

= Graph theory
{parent=Foundations of mathematics}
{wiki}

= Hall marriage theorem
{c}
{parent=Graph theory}
{wiki=Hall%27s_marriage_theorem}

A bipartite graph has a matching saturating $X$ exactly when $|N(S)|\ge|S|$ for every $S\subseteq X$. Sufficiency follows by induction, splitting at a proper tight set when one exists.

= Antichain
{parent=Graph theory}
{wiki}

An antichain is a family of sets no one of which contains another.

= Ramsey theorem
{c}
{parent=Graph theory}
{wiki=Ramsey%27s_theorem}

Every sufficiently large graph contains either a prescribed clique or a prescribed independent set.

= Dirac theorem
{c}
{parent=Graph theory}
{wiki=Dirac%27s_theorem}

Every graph on $n\ge3$ vertices with minimum degree at least $n/2$ has a Hamilton cycle.

= Strongly regular graph
{parent=Graph theory}
{wiki}

A strongly regular graph has constant degree and fixed common-neighbour counts for adjacent and nonadjacent vertex pairs.

= Moser spindle
{c}
{parent=Graph theory}
{wiki}

The Moser spindle is a seven-vertex unit-distance graph with chromatic number four.

= Random graph
{parent=Graph theory}
{wiki}

A random graph is a graph sampled from a probability distribution on graphs.

= Isolated vertex
{parent=Random graph}
{c}
{wiki}

An isolated vertex has degree zero.

= Complement graph
{parent=Graph theory}
{c}
{wiki}

The complement of a simple graph has exactly the edges absent from the original graph; a vertex of degree $d$ acquires degree $n-1-d$.

= Menger theorem
{parent=Graph theory}
{c}
{wiki=Menger%27s_theorem}

The maximum number of disjoint paths joining two vertex sets equals the minimum size of a separating set, with vertex and edge versions according to the chosen notion of disjointness.

= Vertex connectivity
{parent=Menger theorem}
{c}
{wiki=K-vertex-connected_graph}

Vertex connectivity $\kappa(G)$ is the smallest number of vertices whose deletion disconnects a nontrivial graph or reduces it to one vertex.

= Dirac circumference theorem
{parent=Vertex connectivity}
{c}
{wiki=K-vertex-connected_graph#Properties}

A $k$-connected graph has a cycle of length at least $\min\{|G|,2k\}$.

= Longest-cycle attachment argument
{parent=Dirac circumference theorem}
{c}

A component outside a longest cycle has at least $k$ attachment vertices in a $k$-connected graph, and no two attachments are consecutive, forcing cycle length at least $2k$.

= Edge connectivity
{parent=Menger theorem}
{c}
{wiki=K-edge-connected_graph}

Edge connectivity $\lambda(G)$ is the smallest number of edges whose deletion disconnects the graph.

= Whitney inequalities for graph connectivity
{parent=Menger theorem}
{c}
{wiki=Connectivity_(graph_theory)}

For every nontrivial graph, $\kappa(G)\leq\lambda(G)\leq\delta(G)$.

= Connectivity realization construction
{parent=Whitney inequalities for graph connectivity}
{c}

Two large cliques joined by a bipartite cross graph with edge count $\ell$ and vertex-cover number $k$ realize edge connectivity $\ell$ and vertex connectivity $k$ while preserving a chosen minimum degree.

= Bipartite vertex cover
{parent=Graph theory}
{c}
{wiki=Vertex_cover#Bipartite_graphs}

König's theorem equates the minimum vertex-cover size of a bipartite graph with its maximum matching size.

= Statistical learning theory
{parent=Foundations of mathematics}
{wiki}

= Shattering coefficient
{parent=Statistical learning theory}

$s(H,n)$ is the largest number of label vectors induced by $H$ on $n$ points. The VC dimension is the largest $n$ with $s(H,n)=2^n$.

= VC dimension of a vector space
{c}
{parent=Statistical learning theory}
{wiki=VC_dimension}

The sign class of a $d$-dimensional real vector space of functions has VC dimension at most $d$.

= Rademacher complexity
{c}
{parent=Statistical learning theory}
{wiki}

Rademacher complexity measures how strongly a hypothesis class can correlate with independent random signs on a sample.

= Compactness theorem
{c}
{parent=Foundations of mathematics}
{wiki=Compactness_theorem}

A set of first-order sentences has a model if every finite subset has a model.

= Mathematical proof
{parent=Foundations of mathematics}
{wiki=Mathematical_proof}

A mathematical proof is a finite deductive argument from stated assumptions to a conclusion.

= Counterexample
{parent=Mathematical proof}
{c}
{wiki}

A counterexample is one instance satisfying a claim’s hypotheses but not its conclusion, and therefore disproves the universal claim.

= Probability-preserving bijection
{parent=Mathematical proof}
{c}

A probability-preserving bijection pairs outcomes of two events without changing their masses, proving the events have equal probability.

= Analysis
{parent=Mathematics}
{wiki}

= Topological analysis
{parent=Analysis}

= Brouwer fixed-point theorem
{c}
{parent=Topological analysis}

Every continuous self-map of a closed disc has a fixed point.

= No-retraction theorem
{parent=Topological analysis}

No closed disc retracts continuously onto its boundary. This is equivalent to <Brouwer fixed-point theorem> by the standard ray construction and the antipodal map.

= Baire category theorem
{c}
{parent=Topological analysis}
{wiki}

A complete metric space is not a countable union of nowhere-dense closed sets; equivalently, countable intersections of open dense sets are dense.

= Metric space
{parent=Topological analysis}
{wiki}

A metric space is a set equipped with a nonnegative symmetric distance satisfying definiteness and the triangle inequality.

= Distance to a set
{parent=Metric space}
{c}
{wiki=Distance_from_a_point_to_a_set}

For a metric space, $d(x,S)=\inf_{s\in S}d(x,s)$ is $1$-Lipschitz and vanishes on the closure of $S$.

= Functional analysis
{parent=Analysis}
{wiki}

= Open mapping theorem
{parent=Functional analysis}
{wiki}

A bounded surjective linear map between Banach spaces is open. Baire category first puts a ball in the closure of an image; iterative correction removes the closure.

= Closed-range criterion for an injection
{parent=Functional analysis}

A bounded injective map between Banach spaces is bounded below exactly when its image is closed, by the bounded inverse theorem applied to its image.

= Neumann-series perturbation
{c}
{parent=Functional analysis}

If $A$ is invertible and $\|A^{-1}E\|<1$, then $A+E$ is invertible by the Neumann series. Similar iterative corrections prove stability of surjectivity.

= Density by annihilators
{parent=Functional analysis}

A linear subspace is dense exactly when the only continuous functional vanishing on it is zero, by Hahn-Banach separation.

= Riesz representation theorem
{c}
{parent=Functional analysis}
{wiki}

Every bounded linear functional on a Hilbert space is inner product with one unique vector, with equal norms.

= Banach-Alaoglu theorem
{c}
{parent=Functional analysis}
{wiki=Banach–Alaoglu_theorem}

The closed unit ball of a dual Banach space is compact in the weak-star topology.

= Sobolev embedding theorem
{parent=Functional analysis}
{wiki}

If $s>d/2$, then $H^s(\mathbb R^d)$ embeds continuously into bounded continuous functions.

= Space of continuous functions vanishing at infinity
{parent=Functional analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

C0(X) consists of continuous functions whose values become arbitrarily small outside compact sets.

= Noncompactness by separated translates
{parent=Functional analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

Translations of a compactly supported bump can form an infinite family separated by a fixed sup-norm distance, disproving compactness.

= Continuous dual space
{parent=Functional analysis}
{c}
{wiki=Continuous_dual_space}

The continuous dual $X^*$ is the normed space of bounded linear scalar-valued functionals on $X$.

= Operator norm
{parent=Continuous dual space}
{c}
{wiki}

The operator norm is $\lVert T\rVert=\sup_{\lVert x\rVert\leq1}\lVert Tx\rVert$.

= Completeness of the dual space
{parent=Continuous dual space}
{c}

The continuous dual of every normed space is Banach because an operator-norm Cauchy sequence converges pointwise to a bounded linear functional and then uniformly on the unit ball.

= Duality of sequence spaces
{parent=Continuous dual space}
{c}
{wiki=Lp_space#Dual_spaces}

Coordinate pairing gives $(\ell^p)^*=\ell^q$ for $1<p<\infty$, $(\ell^1)^*=\ell^\infty$, and $(c_0)^*=\ell^1$.

= Holder inequality
{parent=Functional analysis}
{c}
{wiki=Hölder%27s_inequality}

For conjugate exponents $p,q$, $\sum_n|x_ny_n|\leq\lVert x\rVert_p\lVert y\rVert_q$.

= Norming vector for Holder inequality
{parent=Holder inequality}
{c}

For nonzero $y\in\ell^q$, a normalized sequence proportional to $\operatorname{sgn}(y_n)|y_n|^{q-1}$ attains equality in Hölder's inequality.

= Space of sequences converging to zero
{parent=Functional analysis}
{c}
{wiki=C0_space#Sequences}

The Banach space $c_0$ consists of scalar sequences tending to zero with the supremum norm.

= Convergent sequence space
{parent=Space of sequences converging to zero}
{c}
{wiki=Sequence_space}

The space $c$ consists of convergent scalar sequences with the supremum norm.

= Absorption of a finite-dimensional summand by c0
{parent=Convergent sequence space}
{c}

The map $(x_n)\mapsto(\lim x_n,x_1-\lim x_n,x_2-\lim x_n,\ldots)$ proves $c\cong c_0$, equivalently $c_0\oplus\mathbb F\cong c_0$.

= Banach space isomorphism
{parent=Functional analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

A Banach space isomorphism is a bounded linear bijection with bounded inverse.

= Fourier analysis
{parent=Analysis}
{wiki}

= Convolution theorem
{parent=Fourier analysis}
{wiki}

For $\widehat f(\xi)=\int f(x)e^{-2\pi ix\cdot\xi}\,dx$, Fubini gives $\widehat{f*g}=\widehat f\,\widehat g$.

= Plancherel theorem
{c}
{parent=Fourier analysis}
{wiki}

The Fourier transform extends unitarily to $L^2$. For Schwartz functions, inversion and Fubini give preservation of inner products.

= Parseval identity
{parent=Fourier analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

= Fourier transform of a Gaussian
{parent=Fourier analysis}
{c}
{wiki=Gaussian_function#Fourier_transform}

With $\widehat f(k)=\int_{\mathbb R}f(x)e^{-ikx}dx$, one has $\widehat{e^{-x^2}}=\sqrt\pi e^{-k^2/4}$.

= Complex analysis
{parent=Analysis}
{wiki}

= Cauchy principal value
{c}
{parent=Complex analysis}
{wiki}

The principal value uses symmetric truncation at infinity and symmetric deletion around real singularities.

= Principal-value beta integral
{parent=Cauchy principal value}
{c}

For $0<s<a$,
$$\operatorname{PV}\int_0^\infty\frac{x^{s-1}}{1-x^a}dx=\frac\pi a\cot\frac{\pi s}{a}.$$

= Principal-value residue rule
{parent=Complex analysis}

For real $a$ and nonzero real $\omega$,
$$
\operatorname{PV}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\frac{e^{i\omega x}}{x-a}\,dx
=i\pi\operatorname{sgn}(\omega)e^{i\omega a}.
$$

= Gamma function
{c}
{parent=Complex analysis}
{wiki}

$\Gamma(z)=\int_0^\infty t^{z-1}e^{-t}\,dt$ for $\Re z>0$, and $\Gamma(z+1)=z\Gamma(z)$ gives meromorphic continuation.

= Gamma reflection formula
{c}
{parent=Gamma function}

$$
\Gamma(z)\Gamma(1-z)=\frac{\pi}{\sin\pi z}.
$$

= Gamma duplication formula
{c}
{parent=Gamma function}

$$
\Gamma(z)\Gamma(z+\tfrac12)=2^{1-2z}\sqrt\pi\,\Gamma(2z).
$$
It follows by making the logarithm of the quotient entire and periodic; growth forces its periodic remainder to be constant.

= Riemann surfaces
{c}
{parent=Complex analysis}
{wiki=Riemann_surface}

= Regular covering map
{parent=Riemann surfaces}
{wiki=Covering_space}

A covering is regular when its deck group acts transitively on each fibre, equivalently when its fundamental-group subgroup is normal.

= Complete analytic function
{parent=Riemann surfaces}

A complete analytic function is the collection of all continuations of a germ. Its Riemann surface consists of these germs, with projection to their base points.

= Riemann-Hurwitz formula
{c}
{parent=Riemann surfaces}
{wiki=Riemann–Hurwitz_formula}

For a degree-$d$ holomorphic map, $2g_X-2=d(2g_Y-2)+\sum(e_p-1)$.

= Weierstrass elliptic function
{c}
{parent=Riemann surfaces}
{wiki=Weierstrass_elliptic_function}

The lattice sum $\wp(z)$ is an even elliptic function with a double pole at every lattice point.

= Riemann sphere
{parent=Riemann surfaces}
{c}
{wiki}

The Riemann sphere is $\mathbb C\cup\{\infty\}$ with two charts related by reciprocal coordinates.

= Stereographic projection
{parent=Riemann sphere}
{c}
{wiki}

Stereographic projection identifies a sphere minus one pole with the plane and supplies the standard charts of the Riemann sphere.

= Punctured Riemann surface
{parent=Riemann surfaces}
{c}

Removing a closed discrete set from a Riemann surface leaves an open complex one-manifold; if connected, it is again a Riemann surface.

= Path avoidance in a surface
{parent=Punctured Riemann surface}
{c}

A path in a surface can be perturbed inside coordinate discs to avoid finitely many prescribed points.

= Punctured complex plane
{parent=Punctured Riemann surface}
{c}
{wiki=Punctured_plane}

The punctured plane $\mathbb C^*$ is a connected Riemann surface homeomorphic to a cylinder.

= Cylinder as a punctured plane
{parent=Punctured complex plane}
{c}

Polar coordinates give $\mathbb C^*\cong S^1\times\mathbb R$ after taking logarithmic radius.

= Transport of a complex structure
{parent=Riemann surfaces}
{c}

A homeomorphism to a complex manifold transports its atlas and thereby defines a complex structure on the source.

= Nodal crossing
{parent=Riemann surfaces}
{c}
{wiki=Node_(algebraic_geometry)}

A nodal crossing locally consists of two complex branches meeting transversely and is not a one-dimensional complex manifold at the intersection.

= Topological manifold local obstruction
{parent=Nodal crossing}
{c}

If a punctured neighborhood has a different number of connected components from a punctured Euclidean ball, the point is not a manifold point.

= Reducible complex curve
{parent=Nodal crossing}
{c}

A reducible complex curve is a union of proper complex subcurves; intersecting components can create singular points.

= Runge theorem
{c}
{parent=Complex analysis}
{wiki=Runge%27s_theorem}

A holomorphic function near a compact set can be uniformly approximated by rational functions with poles in prescribed complementary components; connected complement permits polynomials.

= Winding number
{parent=Complex analysis}
{wiki}

The winding number of a closed path about a missed point is the net change of a continuous argument divided by $2\pi$.

= Homotopy invariance of winding number
{parent=Complex analysis}

A homotopy through closed paths avoiding the base point cannot change the integer winding number.

= Bromwich contour
{c}
{parent=Complex analysis}

A Bromwich contour is a vertical line in the complex frequency plane lying to the right of the singularities in the inverse Laplace integral.

= Branch point
{parent=Complex analysis}
{wiki}

A branch point is a point around which analytic continuation of a multivalued function returns a different value.

= Complex number
{parent=Complex analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

A complex number has the form $x+iy$, with conjugate $x-iy$ and modulus $\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$.

= Removable singularity
{parent=Complex analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

An isolated singularity is removable when the function extends holomorphically across it, equivalently when its Laurent series has no negative-power terms.

= Removable singularity at infinity
{parent=Removable singularity}
{c}
{wiki}

A holomorphic function tending to a finite limit at infinity becomes holomorphic at zero after reciprocal substitution.

= Contour integration
{parent=Complex analysis}
{wiki}

Contour integration integrates complex functions along oriented curves and evaluates many real integrals through residues.

= Jordan lemma
{parent=Contour integration}
{c}
{wiki=Jordan%27s_lemma}

Jordan's lemma controls exponential contour integrals on large semicircles and makes their arc contributions vanish.

= Complex line integral estimate
{parent=Contour integration}
{c}
{wiki}

Integrating along a straight segment bounds a complex integral by segment length times the supremum of the integrand.

= Hankel contour
{parent=Contour integration}
{c}
{wiki}

A Hankel contour runs along both banks of a branch cut and circles its branch point, converting the jump of a complex power into a sine factor.

= Hankel analytic continuation
{parent=Hankel contour}
{c}

Subtracting the local Taylor expansion at the encircled branch point extends a Hankel integral successively across left half-planes; prefactors often remove the introduced poles.

= Residue extraction by a Hankel contour
{parent=Hankel contour}
{c}

When the complex power becomes an integer power, a collapsed Hankel contour extracts the coefficient of $t^{-1}$ in the local Laurent series.

= Univalent function
{parent=Complex analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

A univalent function is a holomorphic injective function.

= Liouville theorem
{parent=Complex analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

Every bounded entire function is constant.

= Polynomial growth theorem for entire functions
{parent=Liouville theorem}
{c}
{wiki}

An entire function bounded by a polynomial in the radius is itself a polynomial, by Cauchy estimates.

= Cauchy derivative formula
{parent=Complex analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

Cauchy’s derivative formula expresses derivatives as contour integrals with higher-order Cauchy kernels.

= Locally uniform convergence of holomorphic functions
{parent=Complex analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

Locally uniform limits of holomorphic functions are holomorphic and their derivatives converge locally uniformly.

= Schwarz reflection principle
{parent=Complex analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

A holomorphic function real on a boundary interval extends across it by conjugate reflection.

= Upper half-plane self-map
{parent=Complex analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

An upper-half-plane self-map is holomorphic and has positive imaginary part in the upper half-plane.

= Argument principle
{parent=Complex analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

The argument principle counts zeros minus poles through the contour integral of the logarithmic derivative.

= Meromorphic function with prescribed zeros and poles
{parent=Complex analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

A meromorphic function with finitely many specified simple zeros and poles is a rational product up to a nonzero holomorphic factor.

= Maximum modulus principle
{parent=Complex analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

A nonconstant holomorphic function on a connected domain cannot attain a local maximum of its modulus at an interior point.

= Mean value property for holomorphic functions
{parent=Complex analysis}
{c}
{wiki=Mean_value_property}

The value of a holomorphic function at the center of a closed disc equals its average around every concentric circle lying in the domain.

= Dirichlet beta function
{parent=Complex analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

The Dirichlet beta function is $\beta(s)=\sum_{n\geq0}(-1)^n(2n+1)^{-s}$ and is the Dirichlet L-function for the nontrivial character modulo four.

= Special values of the Dirichlet beta function
{parent=Dirichlet beta function}
{c}
{wiki=Dirichlet_beta_function#Special_values}

Hankel residue extraction gives $\beta(0)=1/2$, $\beta(-2)=-1/2$, and values at other nonpositive integers from the Taylor coefficients of $1/(2\cosh t)$.

= Trivial zero of a Dirichlet L-function
{parent=Dirichlet beta function}
{c}
{wiki=Dirichlet_L-function#Zeros}

Parity in the functional equation forces the beta function's trivial zeros $\beta(-1)=\beta(-3)=\cdots=0$.

= Dirichlet beta reflection formula
{parent=Dirichlet beta function}
{c}

The functional equation may be written
$$\beta(1-s)=\Gamma(s)(\pi/2)^{-s}\sin(\pi s/2)\beta(s).$$

= Identity theorem
{parent=Complex analysis}
{c}
{wiki=Identity_theorem}

Two holomorphic functions on a connected domain that agree on a set with an interior accumulation point agree everywhere.

= Laplace transform
{parent=Analysis}
{wiki}

The Laplace transform is $\widehat f(p)=\int_0^\infty e^{-pt}f(t)dt$ and converts differentiation into multiplication by $p$ plus initial data.

= Laplace transform shift theorem
{parent=Laplace transform}
{c}
{wiki=Laplace_transform#Time_shifting}

For $a\geq0$, $\mathcal L\{f(t-a)H(t-a)\}(s)=e^{-as}\mathcal L\{f\}(s)$.

= Heaviside step function
{parent=Laplace transform shift theorem}
{c}
{wiki}

The Heaviside function is zero before its switching time and one after it, allowing delayed signals to be written algebraically.

= Laplace transform of a periodic function
{parent=Laplace transform}
{c}

If $g$ has period $T$, then
$$\mathcal L\{g\}(s)=\frac{\int_0^T e^{-st}g(t)dt}{1-e^{-sT}}.$$

= Hardy-Littlewood maximal inequality
{c}
{parent=Analysis}
{wiki=Hardy–Littlewood_maximal_function}

The maximal averaging operator has weak type $(1,1)$, controlling the measure of points where local averages are large.

= Watson lemma
{c}
{parent=Analysis}
{wiki=Watson%27s_lemma}

Watson lemma obtains an endpoint Laplace-integral asymptotic expansion by integrating the local power expansion of its amplitude term by term.

= Stationary phase method
{parent=Analysis}
{wiki}

Oscillatory integrals are led by points where the phase derivative vanishes, with a phase shift determined by the Hessian signature.

= WKB approximation
{c}
{parent=Analysis}
{wiki=WKB_approximation}

For a large-parameter second-order ODE, WKB separates a rapidly varying exponential phase from a slowly varying transport amplitude.

= Numerical analysis
{parent=Analysis}
{wiki}

Numerical analysis studies algorithms for approximating mathematical problems and controlling their errors.

= Gaussian quadrature
{parent=Numerical analysis}
{wiki}

An $n$-node Gaussian quadrature rule uses orthogonal-polynomial roots and integrates every polynomial of degree at most $2n-1$ exactly.

= Chebyshev polynomial
{c}
{parent=Numerical analysis}
{wiki}

$T_n(\cos\theta)=\cos(n\theta)$. These polynomials are orthogonal for weight $(1-x^2)^{-1/2}$ and support spectrally accurate approximation.

= Absolute stability
{parent=Numerical analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

= Backward differentiation formula
{parent=Numerical analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

= Constrained optimization
{parent=Numerical analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

= Equally spaced interpolation
{parent=Numerical analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

= Ford-Fulkerson algorithm
{parent=Numerical analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

= Givens rotation
{parent=Numerical analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

= Gradient descent
{parent=Numerical analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

= Peano kernel theorem
{parent=Numerical analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

The Peano kernel theorem represents a linear approximation error that annihilates low-degree polynomials as an integral against a kernel.

= Runge-Kutta method
{parent=Numerical analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

= Implicit Runge-Kutta method
{parent=Runge-Kutta method}
{c}
{wiki}

An implicit Runge-Kutta method defines one or more stages through equations involving those same stages.

= Trapezoidal rule
{parent=Implicit Runge-Kutta method}
{c}
{wiki}

The trapezoidal ODE rule averages vector fields at the old and new states and is A-stable.

= Order of a Runge-Kutta method
{parent=Runge-Kutta method}
{c}
{wiki}

The order is the highest power through which the one-step expansion matches the exact Taylor expansion.

= Simplex algorithm
{parent=Numerical analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

= Sparse optimization
{parent=Numerical analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

= Stability function
{parent=Numerical analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

= Multistep method
{parent=Numerical analysis}
{wiki=Linear_multistep_method}

A linear multistep method approximates an ODE using several previous solution and derivative values.

= Zero-stability
{parent=Multistep method}
{c}
{wiki}

Zero-stability requires the roots of the first characteristic polynomial to lie in the closed unit disc, with unit-modulus roots simple.

= Root condition for a multistep method
{parent=Zero-stability}
{c}

The root condition is the polynomial criterion equivalent to zero-stability for a linear multistep method.

= Dahlquist equivalence theorem
{parent=Multistep method}
{c}
{wiki}

A consistent linear multistep method is convergent exactly when it is zero-stable.

= Numerical linear algebra
{parent=Numerical analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

Numerical linear algebra develops stable finite algorithms for matrix computations.

= Householder QR decomposition
{parent=Numerical linear algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

Successive Householder reflections zero subdiagonal column entries and factor a matrix into an orthogonal factor and an upper-triangular factor.

= Householder tridiagonalization
{parent=Numerical linear algebra}
{c}
{wiki}

Orthogonal Householder similarities reduce a real symmetric matrix to symmetric tridiagonal form.

= Linear stability domain
{parent=Numerical analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

The linear stability domain is the set of z for which the amplification factor on y-prime=lambda y has modulus at most one.

= A-stability
{parent=Linear stability domain}
{c}
{wiki}

A method is A-stable when its stability domain contains the closed left half-plane.

= Orthogonal polynomial
{parent=Numerical analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

Orthogonal polynomials of distinct degrees are orthogonal under a positive weighted inner product.

= Hermite polynomial
{parent=Orthogonal polynomial}
{c}
{wiki}

= Zeros of orthogonal polynomials
{parent=Orthogonal polynomial}
{c}
{wiki}

A degree-n orthogonal polynomial for a positive interval weight has n simple zeros in the interval interior.

= Jacobi matrix
{parent=Orthogonal polynomial}
{c}
{wiki}

A Jacobi matrix is a symmetric tridiagonal matrix whose characteristic polynomials obey the orthogonal-polynomial three-term recurrence.

= Quadrature rule
{parent=Numerical analysis}
{c}
{wiki=Numerical_integration}

A quadrature rule approximates a weighted integral by a finite weighted sum of function values.

= Moment matching
{parent=Quadrature rule}
{c}

A quadrature rule is exact through degree $d$ precisely when its node weights reproduce the first $d+1$ moments of the integration measure.

= Asymptotic expansion
{parent=Analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

= Bessel function
{parent=Analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

= Branch cut
{parent=Analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

= Cauchy integral formula
{parent=Analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

= Cauchy-Riemann equations
{parent=Analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

= Complex logarithm
{parent=Analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

= Critical point
{parent=Analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

= Curl
{parent=Analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

= Damped harmonic oscillator
{parent=Analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

= Forced harmonic oscillator
{parent=Damped harmonic oscillator}
{c}
{wiki}

A forced harmonic oscillator responds at both its natural and forcing frequencies.

= Differentiable function
{parent=Analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

= Differentiation under the integral sign
{parent=Analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

= Dirichlet problem
{parent=Analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

= Euler-Lagrange equation
{parent=Analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

= Exponential decay
{parent=Analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

= First-step analysis
{parent=Analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

= Forced oscillator
{parent=Analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

= Fourier series
{parent=Analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

= Fourier transform
{parent=Analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

= Sinc-squared integral
{parent=Fourier transform}
{c}
{wiki}

With the standard Fourier convention, the integral of sinc squared over the real line equals pi.

= Fundamental theorem of arithmetic
{parent=Analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

= Gradient flow
{parent=Analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

= Green function
{parent=Analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

= Causal Green function
{parent=Green function}
{c}
{wiki}

A causal Green function vanishes before the source time and has the derivative jump required by a delta source.

= Hermite differential equation
{parent=Analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

= Higher-order Euler-Lagrange equation
{parent=Analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

= Laurent series
{parent=Analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

= Method of characteristics
{parent=Analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

= Ordinary differential equation
{parent=Analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

= Differential inequality
{parent=Ordinary differential equation}
{c}
{wiki}

A differential inequality bounds derivatives rather than specifying them exactly; comparison and integration turn it into bounds on the function.

= Linear ordinary differential equation
{parent=Ordinary differential equation}
{wiki}

A linear ordinary differential equation is linear in the unknown function and its derivatives.

= Second-order linear differential equation
{parent=Linear ordinary differential equation}
{c}

A second-order linear equation has the form $y\prime\prime+p(x)y\prime+q(x)y=f(x)$.

= Power-series solution of a differential equation
{parent=Second-order linear differential equation}
{c}
{wiki}

= Wronskian
{parent=Second-order linear differential equation}
{c}
{wiki}

= Abel identity
{parent=Wronskian}
{c}
{wiki=Abel%27s_identity}

For $y\prime\prime+py\prime+qy=0$, the Wronskian satisfies $W\prime=-pW$.

= Variation of parameters
{parent=Second-order linear differential equation}
{c}
{wiki}

Variation of parameters replaces the constants in a complementary solution by functions to construct a particular solution.

= Reduction of order
{parent=Second-order linear differential equation}
{c}
{wiki}

Given one nonzero solution of a homogeneous second-order linear equation, reduction of order constructs a second by writing it as a variable multiple of the first.

= Cauchy-Euler differential equation
{parent=Second-order linear differential equation}
{c}
{wiki=Cauchy–Euler_equation}

A Cauchy–Euler equation has powers of the independent variable matched to derivative order and is solved using power laws or a logarithmic change of variable.

= Euler-Cauchy equation
{parent=Cauchy-Euler differential equation}
{c}
{wiki}

= Legendre differential equation
{parent=Second-order linear differential equation}
{c}
{wiki=Legendre_differential_equation}

The equation $(1-x^2)y\prime\prime-2xy\prime+\ell(\ell+1)y=0$ has polynomial solutions for nonnegative integer $\ell$.

= Legendre polynomial
{parent=Legendre differential equation}
{c}
{wiki}

The polynomial solution of degree l normalized by P_l(1)=1 is the Legendre polynomial P_l.

= Integrating factor
{parent=Linear ordinary differential equation}
{c}
{wiki}

An integrating factor turns a first-order linear equation into an exact derivative.

= Resonance in a differential equation
{parent=Linear ordinary differential equation}
{c}

Resonance occurs when forcing overlaps a homogeneous mode, requiring multiplication of the usual particular ansatz by an extra power or logarithm.

= Resonant forcing
{parent=Resonance in a differential equation}
{c}
{wiki}

When forcing matches a natural frequency, the oscillator response acquires a linearly growing amplitude.

= Linear system of differential equations
{parent=Linear ordinary differential equation}
{c}
{wiki=Linear_differential_equation#System_of_linear_differential_equations}

A linear differential system has vector form $x\prime=A(t)x+b(t)$.

= Eigenvector method for a differential equation
{parent=Linear system of differential equations}
{c}

For a constant coefficient system, eigenvectors split the homogeneous equation into scalar exponential or power-law modes.

= State transition matrix
{parent=Linear system of differential equations}
{c}
{wiki}

A state transition matrix maps the state of a homogeneous linear system between two times.

= Complex form of a planar linear system
{parent=Linear system of differential equations}
{c}
{wiki}

Encoding two planar components as one complex variable turns a rotation-dilation system into a scalar complex equation.

= Jump condition for an impulse
{parent=Linear ordinary differential equation}
{c}

Integrating an equation through a Dirac impulse determines the jump in the derivative or state while nonsingular lower-order terms contribute no jump.

= First integral
{parent=Ordinary differential equation}
{c}
{wiki}

A first integral is a function of time, state, and derivatives that remains constant along every solution.

= Real analytic function
{parent=Analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

= Residue
{parent=Analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

= Residue theorem
{parent=Analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

= Residue cancellation by symmetry
{parent=Residue theorem}
{c}
{wiki}

Residues at symmetry-related poles can occur with opposite signs and cancel in the contour sum.

= Symmetric residue summation
{parent=Residue theorem}
{c}

Pairing residues at opposite poles can produce an alternating series that converges in a larger half-plane than the corresponding absolutely summed residue series.

= Rouche theorem
{parent=Analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

= Schwarz lemma
{parent=Analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

= Spectral radius
{parent=Analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

= Spherical average
{parent=Analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

= Sturm-Liouville theory
{parent=Analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

= Self-adjoint differential operator
{parent=Sturm-Liouville theory}
{c}
{wiki}

A differential operator is self-adjoint when integration by parts makes its inner products symmetric on its domain.

= Travelling wave
{parent=Analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

= Uniqueness of Poisson equation
{parent=Analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

= Uniqueness theorem for ordinary differential equations
{parent=Analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

= Real analysis
{parent=Analysis}
{wiki}

Real analysis studies limits, continuity, differentiation, integration, and convergence for real-valued functions.

= Lp norm
{parent=Real analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

= Sequence and series
{parent=Real analysis}
{wiki}

A sequence is an ordered family indexed by the natural numbers; a series studies the partial sums of a sequence of terms.

= Bolzano-Weierstrass theorem
{parent=Sequence and series}
{c}
{wiki}

= Convergent sequence
{parent=Sequence and series}
{c}
{wiki}

= Pointwise convergence
{parent=Sequence and series}
{c}
{wiki}

= Power series
{parent=Sequence and series}
{c}
{wiki}

= Radius of convergence
{parent=Power series}
{c}
{wiki}

= Cauchy product
{parent=Power series}
{c}
{wiki}

The Cauchy product has coefficients $c_n=\sum_{k=0}^na_kb_{n-k}$. Absolute convergence permits regrouping and makes its sum the product of the two sums.

= Termwise differentiation of a power series
{parent=Power series}
{c}

Inside its radius of convergence, a power series may be differentiated term by term, and the differentiated series has the same radius.

= Leading Taylor term
{parent=Power series}
{c}
{wiki}

The first nonzero Taylor term controls a holomorphic function’s local magnitude and angular sign pattern.

= Uniform convergence
{parent=Sequence and series}
{c}
{wiki}

= Geometric series
{parent=Sequence and series}
{c}
{wiki}

= Cauchy sequence
{parent=Sequence and series}
{c}
{wiki}

A sequence is Cauchy when its terms become arbitrarily close to one another beyond some index.

= Completeness of the real numbers
{parent=Cauchy sequence}
{c}
{wiki=Completeness_of_the_real_numbers}

Every Cauchy sequence of real numbers converges to a real number.

= Absolute convergence
{parent=Sequence and series}
{c}
{wiki}

A series converges absolutely when the series of absolute values converges; absolute convergence implies convergence.

= Total variation
{parent=Sequence and series}
{c}
{wiki}

The total variation of a sequence is $\sum_n|a_{n+1}-a_n|$; convergence alone does not make it finite.

= Harmonic sum
{parent=Sequence and series}
{c}
{wiki=Harmonic_number}

Harmonic sums satisfy $\sum_{j=n+1}^{2n}1/j\to\log2$ by integral comparison.

= Asymptotic equivalence
{parent=Sequence and series}
{c}
{wiki=Asymptotic_analysis}

The notation $a_n\sim b_n$ means $a_n/b_n\to1$.

= Asymptotic comparison of exponential functions
{parent=Asymptotic equivalence}
{c}
{wiki}

Ratios of exponential functions are controlled by comparing their linear growth exponents.

= Stirling formula
{parent=Asymptotic equivalence}
{c}
{wiki}

Stirling’s formula gives n factorial asymptotic to square root of 2 pi n times (n/e)^n.

= Convergent series
{parent=Sequence and series}
{c}
{wiki}

A series converges when its sequence of partial sums converges to a finite limit.

= Limit comparison test
{parent=Convergent series}
{c}
{wiki}

For positive terms, if the ratio of two sequences tends to a finite positive number, their series either both converge or both diverge.

= Uniform limit
{parent=Sequence and series}
{c}
{wiki}

A sequence converges uniformly when one index makes the approximation accurate at every point of the domain.

= Calculus
{parent=Real analysis}
{wiki}

Calculus studies local change through derivatives and accumulated change through integrals.

= Taylor polynomial
{parent=Calculus}
{c}
{wiki}

= Exponential function
{parent=Calculus}
{c}
{wiki}

= Fundamental theorem of calculus
{parent=Calculus}
{c}
{wiki}

= Mean value theorem
{parent=Calculus}
{c}
{wiki}

= Rolle theorem
{parent=Calculus}
{c}
{wiki}

= Taylor theorem
{parent=Calculus}
{c}
{wiki}

= Limit of a function
{parent=Calculus}
{c}
{wiki=Limit_of_a_function}

The limit records the value approached by a function as its argument approaches a point.

= Continuous function
{parent=Limit of a function}
{c}
{wiki}

A function is continuous at a point when its limit there equals its value.

= Composition of continuous functions
{parent=Continuous function}
{c}

If $f$ is continuous at $x$ and $g$ is continuous at $f(x)$, then $g\circ f$ is continuous at $x$.

= Continuity set of a function
{parent=Continuous function}
{c}

The continuity set of a function is the set of points where it is continuous. For real functions it is always a $G_\delta$ set.

= Uniformly continuous function
{parent=Continuous function}
{c}
{wiki}

A function is uniformly continuous when one input tolerance works at every point of its domain.

= Squeeze theorem
{parent=Limit of a function}
{c}
{wiki}

If two functions with the same limit bound a third nearby, the bounded function has that limit too.

= Derivative
{parent=Calculus}
{c}
{wiki}

The derivative is the limit of the difference quotient and gives the best linear approximation to local change.

= Monotone function
{parent=Calculus}
{c}
{wiki=Monotonic_function}

A function is increasing when $x\le y$ implies $f(x)\le f(y)$.

= Multivariable calculus
{parent=Calculus}
{wiki}

Multivariable calculus extends differentiation and integration to functions of several variables.

= Hessian matrix
{parent=Multivariable calculus}
{c}
{wiki}

The Hessian is the symmetric matrix of second partial derivatives; definiteness classifies nondegenerate critical points.

= Vector calculus
{parent=Multivariable calculus}
{c}
{wiki}

Vector calculus studies differentiation and integration of scalar and vector fields.

= Conservative vector field
{parent=Vector calculus}
{c}
{wiki}

= Divergence theorem
{parent=Vector calculus}
{c}
{wiki}

= Fundamental theorem for line integrals
{parent=Vector calculus}
{c}
{wiki}

= Stokes theorem
{parent=Vector calculus}
{c}
{wiki}

= Surface integral
{parent=Vector calculus}
{c}
{wiki}

A surface integral uses the area element induced by a parametrisation to integrate over a surface.

= Parametrized surface
{parent=Surface integral}
{c}
{wiki}

A parametrised surface $r(u,v)$ has area element $|r_u\times r_v|\,du\,dv$.

= Surface area of a graph
{parent=Surface integral}
{c}
{wiki}

The graph $z=f(x,y)$ has area element $\sqrt{1+f_x^2+f_y^2}\,dx\,dy$.

= Flux integral
{parent=Surface integral}
{c}
{wiki}

A flux integral $\int_SF\cdot n\,dS$ measures flow through an oriented surface.

= Green theorem
{parent=Vector calculus}
{c}
{wiki}

Green’s theorem converts circulation around a positively oriented planar boundary into the area integral of scalar curl.

= Line integral
{parent=Vector calculus}
{c}
{wiki}

A line integral integrates a scalar or tangential vector-field component along a curve.

= Tensor divergence theorem
{parent=Vector calculus}
{c}
{wiki}

Applying the divergence theorem componentwise to $T_{ij}v_i$ yields a boundary traction term and a volume contraction.

= Integration by parts for tensor fields
{parent=Tensor divergence theorem}
{c}
{wiki}

Tensor integration by parts transfers a derivative between tensor factors and introduces the boundary contraction with the outward normal.

= Jacobian matrix
{parent=Multivariable calculus}
{c}
{wiki}

The Jacobian matrix contains all first partial derivatives of a coordinate transformation.

= Change of variables formula
{parent=Multivariable calculus}
{c}
{wiki}

The change-of-variables formula multiplies an integral by the absolute determinant of the Jacobian.

= Polar coordinates
{parent=Change of variables formula}
{c}
{wiki}

Polar coordinates use $x=r\cos\theta$, $y=r\sin\theta$ and area element $r\,dr\,d\theta$.

= Cardioid
{parent=Polar coordinates}
{c}
{wiki}

A cardioid is an epicycloid with one cusp; a standard polar equation is $r=a(1+\cos\theta)$.

= Hyperspherical coordinates
{parent=Change of variables formula}
{c}
{wiki}

Hyperspherical coordinates extend polar coordinates to $n$ dimensions with one radius and $n-1$ angles.

= Volume of an n-ball
{parent=Hyperspherical coordinates}
{c}
{wiki}

The radius-$R$ ball in $\mathbb R^n$ has volume $\pi^{n/2}R^n/\Gamma(n/2+1)$.

= Surface area of an n-sphere
{parent=Hyperspherical coordinates}
{c}
{wiki}

The boundary of the radius-$R$ ball has area $2\pi^{n/2}R^{n-1}/\Gamma(n/2)$.

= Differentiable map
{parent=Multivariable calculus}
{c}
{wiki=Differentiable_function}

A map is differentiable at $x$ when it differs from an affine map with linear part $Df_x$ by $o(\lVert h\rVert)$ at $x+h$.

= Inverse function theorem
{parent=Differentiable map}
{c}
{wiki}

If a continuously differentiable map has invertible derivative at a point, it is a local continuously differentiable diffeomorphism there.

= Symmetry in integration
{parent=Calculus}
{c}
{wiki}

If an integrand is odd under a measure-preserving symmetry of the domain, its integral is zero.

= Integration by parts
{parent=Calculus}
{c}
{wiki}

Integration by parts is the integrated product rule: $\int u\,dv=uv-\int v\,du$.

= Hyperbolic tangent
{parent=Calculus}
{c}
{wiki}

The hyperbolic tangent is sinh divided by cosh and approaches plus or minus one at the two infinities.

= Even function
{parent=Calculus}
{c}
{wiki}

An even function satisfies f(-x)=f(x); its Laurent or Taylor expansion contains only even powers.

= Riemann integration
{parent=Real analysis}
{wiki=Riemann_integral}

Riemann integration approximates area by upper and lower sums over finite partitions.

= Riemann integral
{parent=Riemann integration}
{c}
{wiki}

= Riemann integrability criterion
{parent=Riemann integration}
{c}
{wiki=Riemann_integral}

A bounded function is Riemann integrable exactly when for every $\varepsilon>0$ some partition $P$ satisfies $U(f,P)-L(f,P)<\varepsilon$.

= Darboux sum
{parent=Riemann integration}
{c}
{wiki}

A lower Darboux sum uses the infimum of a function on each partition interval, while an upper sum uses the supremum.

= Convex function
{parent=Real analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

A function is convex when its value on a line segment is at most the corresponding affine interpolation of endpoint values.

= Perspective function
{parent=Convex function}
{c}
{wiki}

The perspective of f is g(t,x)=t f(x/t) for positive t and preserves convexity.

= Subgradient inequality
{parent=Convex function}
{c}
{wiki}

A vector is a subgradient when its supporting affine function lies below the convex function.

= Measure theory
{parent=Real analysis}
{wiki}

Measure theory supplies a rigorous language for size and integration on general spaces.

= Tonelli theorem
{parent=Measure theory}
{c}
{wiki=Fubini%27s_theorem#Tonelli%27s_theorem}

Tonelli’s theorem permits interchange of integrals for a nonnegative measurable function, even before finiteness is known.

= Absolute continuity of measures
{parent=Measure theory}
{c}
{wiki=Absolute_continuity#Absolute_continuity_of_measures}

A measure $\nu$ is absolutely continuous with respect to $\mu$ when every $\mu$-null set is also $\nu$-null.

= Mutually absolutely continuous measures
{parent=Absolute continuity of measures}
{c}

Two measures are mutually absolutely continuous when they have exactly the same null sets.

= Radon-Nikodym theorem
{parent=Absolute continuity of measures}
{c}
{wiki}

For sigma-finite measures, $\nu\ll\mu$ implies $\nu(A)=\int_A(d\nu/d\mu)d\mu$ for a nonnegative measurable density unique almost everywhere.

= Positive Radon-Nikodym derivative
{parent=Radon-Nikodym theorem}
{c}

Mutual absolute continuity is equivalent to the Radon--Nikodym derivative being finite and strictly positive almost everywhere.

= Dominating mixture measure
{parent=Absolute continuity of measures}
{c}

A countable family of probability measures is dominated by any mixture $\sum_nw_n\nu_n$ with every $w_n>0$ and $\sum_nw_n=1$.

= Lp inclusion on a finite measure space
{parent=Measure theory}
{c}
{wiki=Lp_space#Embeddings}

On a finite measure space, $L^q\subseteq L^p$ for $0<p<q\leq\infty$.

= Power singularity integrability
{parent=Lp inclusion on a finite measure space}
{c}

On $(0,1)$, $x^{-a}$ is integrable exactly when $a<1$, providing sharp counterexamples between finite-measure $L^p$ spaces.

= Partial differential equation
{parent=Analysis}
{wiki}

A partial differential equation relates a multivariable function to its partial derivatives.

= Poisson equation
{parent=Partial differential equation}
{c}
{wiki}

= Poisson kernel for the upper half-space
{parent=Poisson equation}
{c}
{wiki}

The upper-half-space Poisson kernel gives the harmonic extension of boundary data.

= Diffusion equation
{parent=Partial differential equation}
{wiki}

A diffusion equation describes smoothing caused by a flux down spatial gradients.

= Nonlinear diffusion equation
{parent=Diffusion equation}
{c}
{wiki=Porous_medium_equation}

A nonlinear diffusion equation lets diffusivity depend on the evolving field; the porous-medium equation is a standard example.

= Similarity solution
{parent=Partial differential equation}
{c}
{wiki=Self-similarity#Self-similar_solutions_of_differential_equations}

A similarity solution combines independent variables into scale-invariant coordinates and reduces a PDE to an ODE.

= Separation of variables
{parent=Partial differential equation}
{c}
{wiki}

Separation of variables seeks a product of one-variable factors, reducing a PDE to ordinary differential equations.

= Polynomial ansatz
{parent=Partial differential equation}
{c}
{wiki}

A polynomial ansatz determines an unknown polynomial solution by matching coefficients after applying the differential operator.

= Harmonic function
{parent=Partial differential equation}
{wiki}

A harmonic function satisfies Laplace's equation $\Delta u=0$.

= Harmonic polynomial
{parent=Harmonic function}
{c}
{wiki}

A harmonic polynomial is a polynomial annihilated by the Laplacian.

= Harmonic conjugate
{parent=Harmonic function}
{c}
{wiki}

A harmonic conjugate v makes u+iv holomorphic; it exists globally on simply connected domains.

= Laplace operator
{parent=Partial differential equation}
{c}
{wiki=Laplacian}

The Laplace operator is the divergence of the gradient, $\Delta=\nabla\cdot\nabla$.

= Radial Laplacian
{parent=Laplace operator}
{c}
{wiki}

For radial $u(r)$ in $n$ dimensions, $\Delta u=u\prime\prime+(n-1)u\prime/r$.

= Laplacian in spherical coordinates
{parent=Laplace operator}
{c}
{wiki}

The spherical-coordinate Laplacian splits into a radial operator and the sphere’s angular Laplacian divided by $r^2$.

= Axisymmetric harmonic function
{parent=Laplacian in spherical coordinates}
{c}
{wiki}

Axisymmetric harmonic functions separate into radial powers times Legendre polynomials.

= Laplacian eigenfunction
{parent=Laplace operator}
{c}
{wiki}

A Laplacian eigenfunction satisfies $-\Delta u=\lambda u$ together with specified boundary conditions.

= Dirichlet Laplacian eigenfunction
{parent=Laplacian eigenfunction}
{c}
{wiki}

A Dirichlet Laplacian eigenfunction vanishes on the boundary and satisfies $-\Delta u=\lambda u$.

= Wave equation
{parent=Partial differential equation}
{c}
{wiki}

The wave equation models finite-speed propagation and second-order oscillation.

= Wave equation on a string
{parent=Wave equation}
{c}
{wiki}

A stretched uniform string obeys y_tt=c^2 y_xx with wave speed equal to the square root of tension over density.

= Normal mode
{parent=Wave equation}
{c}
{wiki}

A normal mode oscillates at one frequency with a fixed spatial eigenfunction.

= Modal energy distribution
{parent=Normal mode}
{c}
{wiki}

Orthogonality separates total quadratic wave energy into a sum of independent modal energies.

= Node of a standing wave
{parent=Normal mode}
{c}
{wiki}

A node is a point where a standing-wave eigenfunction vanishes at all times.

= Fourier transform method for the wave equation
{parent=Wave equation}
{c}

Fourier transformation in space turns $u_{tt}=c^2u_{xx}$ into the independent oscillators $\widehat u_{tt}+c^2k^2\widehat u=0$.

= D'Alembert formula
{parent=Wave equation}
{c}
{wiki=D%27Alembert%27s_formula}

The Cauchy problem $u_{tt}=c^2u_{xx}$ has
$$u(x,t)=\frac{f(x-ct)+f(x+ct)}2+\frac1{2c}\int_{x-ct}^{x+ct}g(y)dy.$$

= Green second identity
{parent=Partial differential equation}
{c}
{wiki}

Green’s second identity is the divergence theorem applied to phi grad psi minus psi grad phi.

= Distribution theory
{parent=Analysis}
{wiki=Distribution_(mathematics)}

Distribution theory extends functions to continuous linear functionals on test functions, allowing generalized derivatives and point sources.

= Dirac delta function
{parent=Distribution theory}
{c}
{wiki}

The Dirac delta is the distribution defined by $\langle\delta,\varphi\rangle=\varphi(0)$.

= Dirac delta scaling
{parent=Dirac delta function}
{c}

For nonzero $a$, $\delta(ax)=\delta(x)/|a|$ in the distributional sense.

= Calculus of variations
{parent=Analysis}
{c}
{wiki}

Calculus of variations finds stationary functions or curves for integral functionals.

= Optical ray in cylindrical coordinates
{parent=Calculus of variations}
{c}
{wiki}

Fermat-type optical paths in cylindrical coordinates extremize refractive index times Euclidean arclength.

= Helical extremal
{parent=Optical ray in cylindrical coordinates}
{c}
{wiki}

A helical extremal has constant radius and a polar angle linear in axial position.

= Noether theorem
{parent=Calculus of variations}
{c}
{wiki}

Noether’s theorem associates a conserved quantity to each continuous variational symmetry.

= Second variation
{parent=Calculus of variations}
{c}
{wiki=Second_variation}

The second variation is the quadratic term in a functional's expansion along $u+\varepsilon\eta$; positivity on admissible variations is a local-minimum test.

= Conjugate point
{parent=Second variation}
{c}
{wiki=Conjugate_points}

A conjugate point marks a nonzero endpoint-vanishing Jacobi variation and the loss of positive definiteness of the second variation.

= Wirtinger inequality
{parent=Second variation}
{c}
{wiki}

For $f(0)=f(T)=0$,
$$\int_0^T|f'|^2dt\geq\frac{\pi^2}{T^2}\int_0^T|f|^2dt,$$
with equality for a multiple of $\sin(\pi t/T)$.

= Fixed-point theorem
{parent=Analysis}
{c}
{wiki=Fixed-point_theorem}

A fixed-point theorem gives conditions under which a map $T$ has a point satisfying $Tx=x$.

= Contraction mapping theorem
{parent=Fixed-point theorem}
{c}
{wiki=Banach_fixed-point_theorem}

A contraction of a nonempty complete metric space has one fixed point, and every orbit converges to it geometrically.

= Iterated contraction
{parent=Contraction mapping theorem}
{c}

If some iterate $T^n$ is a contraction on a complete metric space, then $T$ itself has exactly one fixed point: uniqueness for $T^n$ forces $T$ to fix its fixed point.

= Probability and statistics
{parent=Mathematics}
{wiki}

= Probability theory
{parent=Probability and statistics}
{wiki}

= Borel-Cantelli lemmas
{c}
{parent=Probability theory}
{wiki=Borel–Cantelli_lemma}

If $\sum\mathbb P(A_n)<\infty$, only finitely many $A_n$ occur almost surely. For independent events, divergence implies infinitely many occur. Use a union bound for the first and $\prod(1-p_n)\le e^{-\sum p_n}$ for the second.

= Characteristic-function convergence theorem
{parent=Probability theory}
{wiki=Lévy%27s_continuity_theorem}

Pointwise convergence of characteristic functions to a function continuous at zero implies convergence in distribution.

= Poisson process
{c}
{parent=Probability theory}
{wiki}

A rate-$\lambda$ Poisson process has independent stationary increments. Conditional on $N_t=n$, arrival times are the order statistics of $n$ independent uniform points on $[0,t]$.

= Poisson thinning
{c}
{parent=Poisson process}
{wiki=Thinning_(probability)}

Independently retaining a point at time $s$ with probability $p(s)$ gives a Poisson count of mean $\lambda\int p(s)\,ds$.

= Fokker-Planck equation
{c}
{parent=Probability theory}
{wiki=Fokker–Planck_equation}

A Fokker-Planck equation evolves a density by drift and diffusion in conservation-law form.

= Wald identity
{c}
{parent=Probability theory}
{wiki=Wald%27s_equation}

For an integrable stopping time and i.i.d. integrable increments, the expected stopped sum equals expected stopping time times mean increment.

= Poisson point process
{parent=Probability theory}
{wiki}

A Poisson point process has independent counts on disjoint sets and Poisson count in each set with mean given by its intensity measure.

= Order statistic
{parent=Probability theory}
{c}
{wiki}

= Order symmetry of independent random variables
{parent=Order statistic}
{c}

Independent identically distributed continuous variables realize each of their possible strict orderings with equal probability.

= Probability distribution
{parent=Probability theory}
{wiki}

A probability distribution assigns probabilities to the possible values of a random variable.

= Discrete probability distribution
{parent=Probability distribution}
{wiki}

A discrete probability distribution is supported on a finite or countable set.

= Binomial distribution
{parent=Discrete probability distribution}
{c}
{wiki}

The binomial distribution counts successes in independent Bernoulli trials with common success probability.

= Poisson distribution
{parent=Discrete probability distribution}
{c}
{wiki}

The Poisson distribution with mean lambda assigns mass $e^{-\lambda}\lambda^k/k!$.

= Poisson-multinomial conditioning
{parent=Poisson distribution}
{c}
{wiki}

Independent Poisson counts conditioned on their sum have a multinomial distribution.

= Continuous probability distribution
{parent=Probability distribution}
{wiki}

A continuous probability distribution is described by a density with respect to Lebesgue measure.

= Gamma distribution
{parent=Continuous probability distribution}
{c}
{wiki}

= Uniform distribution
{parent=Continuous probability distribution}
{c}
{wiki=Continuous_uniform_distribution}

A continuous uniform distribution has constant density on its interval of support.

= Laplace distribution
{parent=Continuous probability distribution}
{c}
{wiki}

The centred Laplace distribution with rate $\theta$ has density $\theta e^{-\theta|x|}/2$.

= Symmetric distribution
{parent=Probability distribution}
{c}

A distribution symmetric about $c$ is invariant under $x\mapsto2c-x$; when its mean exists, it equals $c$.

= Median
{parent=Probability distribution}
{c}
{wiki=Median}

A median is a point with at least half the probability on either side.

= Moment-generating function
{parent=Probability distribution}
{c}
{wiki}

The moment-generating function is $M_X(t)=E[e^{tX}]$ where finite and encodes moments through derivatives at zero.

= Joint distribution function
{parent=Probability distribution}
{c}

The joint distribution function is $F(x,y)=P(X\le x,Y\le y)$; mixed differentiation gives a joint density when sufficiently smooth.

= Normal distribution
{parent=Probability distribution}
{c}
{wiki}

The normal distribution has density proportional to $\exp(-(x-\mu)^2/(2\sigma^2))$.

= Standard normal distribution
{parent=Normal distribution}
{c}
{wiki}

The standard normal distribution is $N(0,1)$ and has cumulative distribution function $\Phi$.

= Chi-squared distribution
{parent=Probability distribution}
{c}
{wiki}

A $\chi_k^2$ variable is the sum of squares of $k$ independent standard normal variables.

= Chi-squared stochastic ordering
{parent=Chi-squared distribution}
{c}

If $r<s$, coupling by extra independent squared normals gives $\chi_s^2=\chi_r^2+W$ with $W\geq0$, so every fixed upper quantile increases with the degrees of freedom.

= Weibull distribution
{parent=Probability distribution}
{c}
{wiki}

The Weibull distribution has density $k\lambda^{-1}(y/\lambda)^{k-1}e^{-(y/\lambda)^k}$ on $y>0$.

= Characteristic function of a uniform distribution
{parent=Probability distribution}
{c}

For $X$ uniform on $[-a,a]$, $\phi_X(t)=\sin(at)/(at)$.

= Small-argument sine product asymptotic
{parent=Characteristic function of a uniform distribution}
{c}

The expansion $\log(\sin x/x)=-x^2/6+O(x^4)$ converts products of small sinc factors into Gaussian characteristic-function limits.

= Variance of a uniform distribution
{parent=Probability distribution}
{c}

A uniform variable on $[-a,a]$ has mean zero and variance $a^2/3$.

= Expected value
{parent=Probability theory}
{wiki}

The expected value is the probability-weighted average of a random variable, defined by a sum or an integral when it exists.

= Indicator random variable
{parent=Expected value}
{c}
{wiki}

= Tail-sum formula
{parent=Expected value}
{c}

For nonnegative $Y$, $E[Y]=\int_0^\infty P(Y\ge t)\,dt$; for nonnegative integer $N$, $E[N]=\sum_{k\ge1}P(N\ge k)$.

= Linearity of expectation
{parent=Expected value}
{c}
{wiki=Expected_value#Linearity}

Expectation is linear without requiring independence: $E[aX+bY]=aE[X]+bE[Y]$ whenever the expectations exist.

= Variance of a sample mean
{parent=Expected value}
{c}

For independent identically distributed variables of variance $\sigma^2$, the mean of $n$ samples has variance $\sigma^2/n$.

= Probability inequality
{parent=Probability theory}
{wiki=Concentration_inequality}

Probability inequalities bound event probabilities using moments or other tractable quantities.

= Chebyshev inequality
{parent=Probability inequality}
{c}
{wiki}

= Markov inequality
{parent=Probability inequality}
{c}
{wiki}

= Union bound
{parent=Probability inequality}
{c}
{wiki=Boole%27s_inequality}

The union bound says $P(\bigcup_i A_i)\le\sum_iP(A_i)$.

= Convergence of random variables
{parent=Probability theory}
{wiki}

Convergence of random variables includes almost sure, probability, distributional, and mean convergence.

= Central limit theorem
{parent=Convergence of random variables}
{c}
{wiki}

= Poisson limit theorem
{parent=Convergence of random variables}
{c}
{wiki=Poisson_limit_theorem}

Binomial distributions with trial count tending to infinity and success probability tending to zero converge to a Poisson law when their product converges.

= Conditional probability
{parent=Probability theory}
{c}
{wiki}

Conditional probability is $P(A\mid B)=P(A\cap B)/P(B)$ when $P(B)>0$.

= Conditional distribution
{parent=Conditional probability}
{c}
{wiki}

A conditional distribution is the probability law of one random quantity given information about another.

= Random partition
{parent=Probability theory}
{wiki}

A random partition is a probability distribution on partitions of a finite or countable set.

= Restricted-growth string
{parent=Random partition}
{c}

A restricted-growth string encodes a set partition by numbering blocks in order of first appearance.

= Singleton block
{parent=Random partition}
{c}

A singleton block is a block containing exactly one element.

= Exchangeable random partition
{parent=Random partition}
{c}
{wiki=Exchangeable_random_partition}

An exchangeable random partition has a law invariant under finite permutations of the underlying labels.

= Random path in a binary tree
{parent=Probability theory}
{c}

In a fair random root-to-leaf path, a specified leaf at depth $d$ is reached with probability $2^{-d}$.

= Markov chain
{parent=Probability theory}
{c}
{wiki}

A Markov chain has a future conditional distribution depending on the present state alone.

= Reversible Markov chain
{parent=Markov chain}
{c}
{wiki}

A Markov chain is reversible when its stationary flow satisfies detailed balance.

= Detailed balance
{parent=Reversible Markov chain}
{c}
{wiki}

Detailed balance is the identity pi_i P_ij = pi_j P_ji for every pair of states.

= Hitting probability
{parent=Markov chain}
{c}
{wiki}

A hitting probability is the probability of reaching one set before another and solves a discrete harmonic boundary problem.

= Conditional hitting time
{parent=Markov chain}
{c}
{wiki}

A conditional hitting time measures time to a target under a specified successful hitting event and can be computed by weighted first-step equations.

= Periodic Markov chain
{parent=Markov chain}
{c}
{wiki}

A periodic chain can return to a state only at times sharing a common divisor greater than one.

= Lazy Markov chain
{parent=Markov chain}
{c}
{wiki}

A lazy chain stays put with positive probability, removing periodicity without changing invariant distributions.

= Convergence to an invariant distribution
{parent=Markov chain}
{c}
{wiki}

A finite irreducible aperiodic Markov chain converges to its unique invariant distribution.

= Recurrent Markov chain
{parent=Markov chain}
{c}
{wiki}

A state is recurrent when the chain returns to it almost surely after starting there.

= Recurrence criterion by return probabilities
{parent=Recurrent Markov chain}
{c}
{wiki}

A state is recurrent exactly when the sum over time of its return probabilities diverges.

= Finite Markov chain
{parent=Markov chain}
{c}
{wiki}

= Communicating class
{parent=Markov chain}
{c}
{wiki}

= Closed communicating class
{parent=Communicating class}
{c}

A communicating class is closed when no transition from it can reach a state outside it.

= Transient communicating class
{parent=Communicating class}
{c}

In a finite chain, a communicating class that is not closed is transient: after it is left, it need not be visited again and is eventually left almost surely.

= Stationary distribution
{parent=Markov chain}
{c}
{wiki}

A stationary distribution $\pi$ satisfies $\pi P=\pi$.

= Renewal process
{parent=Probability theory}
{c}
{wiki}

A renewal process records successive sums of independent positive lifetimes.

= Residual lifetime process
{parent=Renewal process}
{c}
{wiki}

The residual lifetime is the time remaining until the next renewal.

= Residual lifetime Markov chain
{parent=Residual lifetime process}
{c}
{wiki}

With bounded integer lifetimes, residual lifetime decreases deterministically and resets randomly at zero.

= Equilibrium residual-life distribution
{parent=Residual lifetime process}
{c}
{wiki}

The invariant residual-life mass at i is proportional to the probability that a lifetime exceeds i.

= Law of total variance
{parent=Probability theory}
{c}
{wiki}

Variance equals the variance of a conditional mean plus the mean conditional variance.

= Characteristic function of a sum of independent variables
{parent=Probability theory}
{c}
{wiki=Characteristic_function_(probability_theory)}

Independence makes the characteristic function of a sum equal the product of the individual characteristic functions.

= Variance normalization
{parent=Probability theory}
{c}

Dividing a centered sum by the square root of its total variance gives unit variance and is the natural central-limit scaling.

= Information theory
{parent=Probability and statistics}
{wiki}

= Huffman coding
{c}
{parent=Information theory}
{wiki}

Huffman's algorithm repeatedly combines the $D$ least weights for a $D$-ary prefix code and yields an optimal expected length.

= Huffman sibling property
{parent=Huffman coding}
{c}

Some optimal prefix tree places the two least probable symbols as sibling leaves of maximum depth, which is the inductive basis of Huffman's algorithm.

= Optimal prefix code
{parent=Huffman coding}
{c}
{wiki=Huffman_coding}

An optimal prefix code minimizes probability-weighted codeword length among all prefix codes for the source.

= Nonunique optimal prefix code
{parent=Optimal prefix code}
{c}

Distinct tree shapes can have the same minimum expected length, particularly when Huffman merges have ties.

= Huffman codeword length bounds
{parent=Huffman coding}
{c}

For a largest binary-symbol probability $p_1$, $p_1<1/3$ prevents a length-one word, while $p_1>2/5$ forces one in a Huffman tree.

= Bernoulli source
{parent=Information theory}

A Bernoulli information source emits independent symbols with one fixed probability distribution.

= Information rate
{parent=Information theory}
{wiki}

The information rate is the limiting entropy per emitted symbol, when that limit exists.

= Asymptotic equipartition property
{parent=Information theory}
{wiki}

For a stationary source, typical long blocks have probability approximately $2^{-nH}$; equivalently normalized self-information converges to the entropy rate.

= Shannon noiseless coding theorem
{c}
{parent=Information theory}
{wiki=Shannon%27s_source_coding_theorem}

For binary prefix codes, the optimal expected length $L$ obeys $H(X)\le L<H(X)+1$.

= Statistical modelling
{parent=Probability and statistics}
{wiki=Statistical_model}

= Exponential family
{parent=Statistical modelling}
{wiki}

A density $h(y)\exp\{\theta T(y)-A(\theta)\}$ has sufficient statistic $T$ and cumulant function $A$.

= Independent Poisson conditioning
{parent=Statistical modelling}

Independent Poisson variables conditioned on their sum are multinomial, with cell probabilities proportional to their rates.

= Normal linear model
{parent=Statistical modelling}

$Y=X\beta+\varepsilon$, $\varepsilon\sim N(0,\sigma^2I)$, and full-rank $X$ gives $\widehat\beta=(X^TX)^{-1}X^TY$.

= Gauss-Markov theorem
{parent=Normal linear model}
{c}
{wiki}

Among linear unbiased estimators in a homoscedastic linear model, least squares has minimum variance.

= Weighted least squares
{parent=Normal linear model}
{c}
{wiki}

Weighted least squares minimizes $\sum_iw_i(Y_i-X_i^T\beta)^2$; inverse-variance weights give the efficient estimator under known heteroscedasticity.

= Generalized least squares
{parent=Weighted least squares}
{c}
{wiki}

Generalized least squares minimizes $(Y-X\beta)^T\Omega^{-1}(Y-X\beta)$ for a known covariance shape $\Omega$.

= Whitening transformation
{parent=Generalized least squares}
{c}
{wiki=Whitening_transformation}

Whitening multiplies a model by a square root of its precision matrix so that the transformed errors have identity covariance.

= Normal equation
{parent=Normal linear model}
{c}
{wiki=Linear_least_squares#Derivation_of_the_normal_equations}

Least-squares differentiation gives $X^TX\widehat\beta=X^TY$, or its precision-weighted analogue.

= Consistency of least squares
{parent=Normal linear model}
{c}

Under finite moments and a nonsingular limiting design moment, laws of large numbers make least-squares estimators converge to the true coefficient.

= Regression leverage
{parent=Statistical modelling}

For $H=X(X^TX)^{-1}X^T$, leverage is $h_{ii}$ and residual variance is $\sigma^2(1-h_{ii})$.

= Log-linear model
{parent=Statistical modelling}

Additive main effects encode mutual independence in contingency-table Poisson models. An interaction permits association of its factors.

= Maximum-likelihood estimator
{parent=Statistical modelling}
{wiki=Maximum_likelihood_estimation}

An MLE maximizes sample likelihood. Regularly, $\sqrt n(\widehat\theta-\theta)\Rightarrow N(0,I(\theta)^{-1})$; parameter-dependent support can change both rate and limit.

= Gaussian conditional expectation
{c}
{parent=Statistical modelling}
{wiki=Conditional_expectation}

For jointly Gaussian $(S,Y)$ with $\operatorname{cov}(S)=V$,
$$
\mathbb E[Y\mid S]=\mathbb EY+\operatorname{cov}(Y,S)V^{-1}(S-\mathbb ES).
$$

= Logistic regression
{parent=Statistical modelling}
{wiki}

Logistic regression models the conditional log odds as an affine function of predictors.

= Iteratively reweighted least squares
{c}
{parent=Statistical modelling}
{wiki}

IRLS fits a generalized linear model by repeatedly solving a weighted least-squares approximation to its score equations.

= Linear discriminant analysis
{c}
{parent=Statistical modelling}
{wiki}

LDA assumes class-conditional Gaussian distributions with a shared covariance matrix, producing affine log odds.

= Bootstrap
{parent=Statistical modelling}
{wiki}

The bootstrap approximates a sampling distribution by repeatedly sampling with replacement from the empirical distribution.

= Cramer-Rao bound
{c}
{parent=Statistical modelling}
{wiki=Cramér–Rao_bound}

The Cramer-Rao inequality lower-bounds estimator variance by inverse Fisher information, with a derivative correction for bias.

= Statistical hypothesis testing
{parent=Statistical modelling}
{wiki=Statistical_hypothesis_test}

Statistical hypothesis testing compares data against a null hypothesis using a controlled rejection probability.

= Likelihood-ratio test
{parent=Statistical hypothesis testing}
{c}
{wiki}

A likelihood-ratio test rejects where the alternative likelihood is sufficiently large relative to the null likelihood.

= Generalized likelihood-ratio test
{parent=Likelihood-ratio test}
{c}
{wiki=Likelihood-ratio_test#Generalized_likelihood-ratio}

A generalized likelihood-ratio test compares the likelihood maximized under the null with the likelihood maximized over the full parameter space.

= Likelihood-ratio test for equality of two normal means
{parent=Generalized likelihood-ratio test}
{c}

For independent unit-variance samples, testing equal means uses $Z=\sqrt{mn/(m+n)}(\bar X-\bar Y)$ and rejects for large $|Z|$.

= Nested likelihood-ratio rejection regions
{parent=Generalized likelihood-ratio test}
{c}

Likelihood-ratio tests of different null subspaces can have crossing rejection regions; comparing their quadratic-form geometry exhibits observations accepted by one and rejected by another.

= Neyman-Pearson lemma
{parent=Statistical hypothesis testing}
{c}
{wiki}

= Augmented Markov chain
{parent=Probability and statistics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Bayesian normal model
{parent=Probability and statistics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Branching-process extinction criterion
{parent=Probability and statistics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Cauchy-Schwarz inequality
{parent=Probability and statistics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Cover time
{parent=Probability and statistics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Expected energy
{parent=Probability and statistics}
{c}
{wiki}

= F-test
{parent=Probability and statistics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Galton-Watson process
{parent=Probability and statistics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Gambler ruin
{parent=Probability and statistics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Gaussian tail bound
{parent=Probability and statistics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Gaussian wave packet
{parent=Probability and statistics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Geometric distribution
{parent=Probability and statistics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Gronwall inequality
{parent=Probability and statistics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Kac lemma
{parent=Probability and statistics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Markov additive process
{parent=Probability and statistics}
{c}
{wiki}

A Markov additive process couples a Markov chain to an additive functional whose increments depend on the chain.

= Multiple hypothesis testing
{parent=Probability and statistics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Multivariate normal distribution
{parent=Probability and statistics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Orthogonal projection of a Gaussian vector
{parent=Multivariate normal distribution}
{c}
{wiki}

Orthogonal projections of an isotropic Gaussian vector onto orthogonal subspaces are independent.

= Paley-Zygmund inequality
{parent=Probability and statistics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Pivotal quantity
{parent=Probability and statistics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Probability-generating function
{parent=Probability and statistics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Random walk on a graph
{parent=Probability and statistics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Stationary distribution of a graph random walk
{parent=Random walk on a graph}
{c}
{wiki}

On a finite undirected graph, stationary mass is proportional to vertex degree.

= Biased random walk
{parent=Random walk on a graph}
{c}
{wiki}

A biased nearest-neighbor walk steps right and left with unequal probabilities.

= Rao-Blackwell theorem
{parent=Probability and statistics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Regression residual
{parent=Probability and statistics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Strong Markov property
{parent=Probability and statistics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Sufficient statistic
{parent=Probability and statistics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Fisher-Neyman factorization theorem
{parent=Sufficient statistic}
{c}
{wiki}

A statistic is sufficient exactly when the likelihood factors into a parameter-dependent function of the statistic times a parameter-free function of the sample.

= Weierstrass M-test
{parent=Probability and statistics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Statistical inference
{parent=Probability and statistics}
{c}
{wiki}

Statistical inference estimates unknown parameters and quantifies uncertainty from observed data.

= Student t confidence interval
{parent=Statistical inference}
{c}
{wiki}

A Student t confidence interval replaces unknown Gaussian scale by an independent residual estimate.

= Bayesian statistics
{parent=Statistical inference}
{c}
{wiki}

Bayesian statistics updates a prior distribution to a posterior distribution using likelihood.

= Bayesian posterior
{parent=Bayesian statistics}
{c}
{wiki}

The posterior density is proportional to likelihood times prior density.

= Gamma-exponential conjugacy
{parent=Bayesian posterior}
{c}
{wiki}

A gamma prior combined with exponential observations gives a gamma posterior.

= Bayes estimator
{parent=Bayesian statistics}
{c}
{wiki}

A Bayes estimator minimizes posterior expected loss.

= Bayes estimator under squared error loss
{parent=Bayes estimator}
{c}
{wiki}

Under squared error loss, the Bayes estimator is the posterior mean.

= Posterior expected loss
{parent=Bayes estimator}
{c}
{wiki}

Posterior expected loss averages the loss over the posterior and is minimized pointwise in the data.

= Asymptotic normality
{parent=Statistical inference}
{c}
{wiki}

An estimator is asymptotically normal when a rescaled estimation error converges in distribution to a normal law.

= Slutsky theorem
{parent=Asymptotic normality}
{c}
{wiki}

Slutsky's theorem combines convergence in distribution with convergence in probability through continuous algebraic operations.

= Sandwich covariance matrix
{parent=Asymptotic normality}
{c}
{wiki=Heteroskedasticity-consistent_standard_errors}

For estimating equations, asymptotic covariance often has the form $A^{-1}BA^{-1}$, called a sandwich covariance.

= Asymptotic relative efficiency
{parent=Statistical inference}
{c}
{wiki}

Asymptotic relative efficiency compares the leading asymptotic variances of two consistent estimators.

= Mathematical physics
{parent=Mathematics}
{wiki}

= Classical mechanics
{parent=Mathematical physics}
{wiki}

= Legendre transform in mechanics
{c}
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{wiki=Legendre_transformation}

For a regular Lagrangian, $p=\partial L/\partial\dot q$ and $H=p\dot q-L$. Regularity means the velocity Hessian is invertible.

= Hamiltonian of a charged particle
{c}
{parent=Classical mechanics}

For $L=m\dot r^2/2-e\phi+e\dot r\cdot A$, $p=m\dot r+eA$ and $H=|p-eA|^2/(2m)+e\phi$.

= Virial theorem
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{wiki}

For a confined Newtonian gravitational system, $\dot G=2T+V$ for $G=\sum p_i\cdot r_i$, hence $2\langle T\rangle=-\langle V\rangle$.

= Integrable Hamiltonian system
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{wiki=Integrable_system}

Liouville integrability means having $n$ almost-everywhere independent first integrals in mutual Poisson involution.

= Arnold-Liouville theorem
{c}
{parent=Integrable Hamiltonian system}
{wiki=Liouville–Arnold_theorem}

A compact connected regular common level of $n$ commuting integrals is an $n$-torus with local action-angle coordinates.

= Liouville theorem in Hamiltonian mechanics
{c}
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{wiki=Liouville%27s_theorem_(Hamiltonian)}

Hamiltonian flow has zero phase-space divergence and preserves phase volume.

= Poincare recurrence theorem
{c}
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{wiki=Poincaré_recurrence_theorem}

A finite measure-preserving dynamical system returns almost every point arbitrarily close to its start infinitely often.

= Particle dynamics
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{c}
{wiki}

Particle dynamics studies motion determined by forces through Newton’s laws.

= One-dimensional conservative motion
{parent=Particle dynamics}
{c}
{wiki}

For force minus the derivative of a potential, total kinetic plus potential energy is conserved.

= Period near a separatrix
{parent=One-dimensional conservative motion}
{c}
{wiki}

A trajectory approaching a nondegenerate unstable potential maximum has a logarithmically divergent period.

= Collision mechanics
{parent=Particle dynamics}
{c}
{wiki}

Collision mechanics uses impulse together with conservation of momentum and, for elastic collisions, kinetic energy.

= Elastic collision
{parent=Collision mechanics}
{c}
{wiki}

An elastic collision conserves total momentum and total kinetic energy.

= Oblique collision
{parent=Collision mechanics}
{c}
{wiki}

In a frictionless oblique collision, impulse acts along the common normal and tangential velocity components remain unchanged.

= Impulse
{parent=Collision mechanics}
{c}
{wiki}

Impulse is the time integral of force and equals the change in linear momentum.

= Celestial mechanics
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{c}
{wiki}

Celestial mechanics studies motion under gravitational forces.

= Kepler orbit
{parent=Celestial mechanics}
{c}
{wiki}

An inverse-square central force produces conic-section trajectories with the force centre at a focus.

= Binet equation
{parent=Kepler orbit}
{c}
{wiki}

Binet’s equation rewrites a central-force orbit using reciprocal radius as a function of polar angle.

= Orbital eccentricity
{parent=Kepler orbit}
{c}
{wiki}

Orbital eccentricity distinguishes circular, elliptic, parabolic, and hyperbolic conics.

= Hyperbolic Kepler orbit
{parent=Kepler orbit}
{c}
{wiki}

A positive-energy inverse-square orbit is a hyperbola with two scattering asymptotes.

= Gravitational scattering angle
{parent=Hyperbolic Kepler orbit}
{c}
{wiki}

The gravitational scattering angle is the change between incoming and outgoing asymptotic velocity directions.

= Parabolic Kepler orbit
{parent=Kepler orbit}
{c}
{wiki}

A zero-energy inverse-square orbit is parabolic and has eccentricity one.

= Barker equation
{parent=Parabolic Kepler orbit}
{c}
{wiki}

Barker’s equation relates time on a parabolic orbit to the tangent of half the true anomaly through a cubic.

= Rigid body dynamics
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{c}
{wiki}

Rigid body dynamics studies translation and rotation of bodies whose internal distances are fixed.

= Moment of inertia
{parent=Rigid body dynamics}
{c}
{wiki}

Moment of inertia is the mass-weighted squared distance from a rotation axis and determines rotational kinetic energy.

= Parallel axis theorem
{parent=Moment of inertia}
{c}
{wiki}

The moment about a parallel axis equals the centre-of-mass moment plus mass times the squared axis separation.

= Rolling without slipping
{parent=Rigid body dynamics}
{c}
{wiki}

Rolling without slipping imposes that the contact point is instantaneously at rest, giving v=R omega.

= Lagrangian mechanics
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{c}
{wiki}

Lagrangian mechanics derives motion from the stationary action of $L=T-V$ through the Euler--Lagrange equations.

= Center-of-mass motion
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{c}
{wiki=Center_of_mass}

The center of mass moves as if the total external force acted on the total mass; internal forces cancel in its equation.

= Center-of-mass reduction
{parent=Center-of-mass motion}
{c}

Center-of-mass reduction separates translational motion from internal coordinates by using the conserved or externally forced center coordinate.

= Uniform gravitational field
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{c}

In a uniform gravitational field, every freely falling center of mass has constant acceleration $-g$.

= Normal coordinate
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{c}
{wiki=Normal_mode}

A normal coordinate diagonalizes the linearized kinetic and potential forms so that its motion decouples as a harmonic oscillator.

= Antisymmetric molecular vibration
{parent=Normal coordinate}
{c}

In an antisymmetric vibration of a symmetric linear triatomic molecule, the outer atoms move oppositely and the central atom does not couple to that mode.

= Dynamical systems
{parent=Mathematical physics}
{wiki=Dynamical_system}

= Strict Lyapunov function
{c}
{parent=Dynamical systems}
{wiki=Lyapunov_function}

$V(x_0)=0$, $V>0$ elsewhere, and $\dot V<0$ prove asymptotic stability; suitable sublevel sets lie in the basin.

= Stable manifold
{parent=Dynamical systems}
{wiki}

At a hyperbolic fixed point, stable and unstable manifolds are tangent to the corresponding eigenspaces. Power-series coefficients follow from graph invariance.

= Poincare-Bendixson theorem
{c}
{parent=Dynamical systems}
{wiki=Poincaré–Bendixson_theorem}

A compact planar limit set containing no equilibrium is a periodic orbit.

= Floquet multiplier
{c}
{parent=Dynamical systems}
{wiki}

A Floquet multiplier is an eigenvalue of the derivative of a period map and determines transverse stability of a periodic orbit.

= Fixed point stability for an autonomous differential equation
{parent=Dynamical systems}
{c}

For $x\prime=f(x)$, a simple fixed point $x_*$ is locally asymptotically stable when $f\prime(x_*)<0$ and unstable when $f\prime(x_*)>0$.

= Fixed point stability for an iteration
{parent=Dynamical systems}
{c}

A fixed point $x_*$ of $x_{n+1}=g(x_n)$ is locally asymptotically stable when $|g\prime(x_*)|<1$.

= Resonance
{parent=Dynamical systems}
{c}
{wiki=Resonance}

Resonance is the large response produced when periodic forcing aligns with a natural mode.

= Unstable equilibrium
{parent=Dynamical systems}
{c}
{wiki}

An equilibrium is unstable when arbitrarily small perturbations can move trajectories away from it.

= Equilibrium of an autonomous differential equation
{parent=Dynamical systems}
{c}
{wiki=Equilibrium_point}

An equilibrium is a state where the autonomous vector field vanishes.

= Linear stability analysis
{parent=Equilibrium of an autonomous differential equation}
{c}
{wiki=Linear_stability}

Linear stability analysis classifies a hyperbolic equilibrium from the eigenvalues of the vector field's Jacobian.

= Saddle equilibrium
{parent=Linear stability analysis}
{c}
{wiki=Saddle_point}

A saddle equilibrium has both stable and unstable linear directions and is therefore unstable.

= Basin of attraction
{parent=Dynamical systems}
{c}
{wiki}

The basin of an attractor is the set of initial states whose forward trajectories converge to it.

= Mathematical biology
{parent=Mathematical physics}
{wiki}

= Epidemic threshold
{parent=Mathematical biology}

A cross-infection model's disease-free equilibrium becomes a saddle when the product of infection gains exceeds the product of recovery rates.

= Kramers-Moyal expansion
{c}
{parent=Mathematical biology}
{wiki=Kramers–Moyal_expansion}

The Kramers-Moyal expansion turns a jump-process master equation into a derivative expansion; second-order truncation is the Fokker-Planck equation.

= Compartmental model
{parent=Mathematical biology}
{c}
{wiki=Compartmental_models_in_epidemiology}

A compartmental model divides a conserved population into states and uses differential equations for transition rates.

= Mass-action interaction
{parent=Compartmental model}
{c}

Under mass action, random pairwise encounters between populations of sizes $X$ and $Y$ occur at a rate proportional to $XY$.

= Cosmology
{parent=Mathematical physics}
{wiki}

= Friedmann equation
{c}
{parent=Cosmology}
{wiki=Friedmann_equations}

$H=\dot a/a$, a fluid with $P=w\rho$ has $\rho\propto a^{-3(1+w)}$, and $\rho_{\rm crit}=3c^2H^2/(8\pi G)$.

= Phantom energy
{parent=Cosmology}
{wiki}

A fluid with $w<-1$ grows in density during expansion and can produce a finite-time big-rip singularity.

= Scale factor
{parent=Cosmology}
{c}
{wiki=Scale_factor_(cosmology)}

The cosmological scale factor $a(t)$ converts comoving separations into physical separations in an FLRW universe.

= Comoving coordinate
{parent=Scale factor}
{c}
{wiki=Comoving_and_proper_distances}

A comoving coordinate remains fixed for an observer following the homogeneous cosmological expansion.

= Cosmological redshift
{parent=Scale factor}
{c}
{wiki}

Light emitted at $t_e$ and observed at $t_0$ has $1+z=a(t_0)/a(t_e)$.

= Cosmological time dilation
{parent=Cosmological redshift}
{c}

Observed time intervals from a comoving source are stretched by the same factor $1+z$ as photon wavelengths.

= Luminosity distance
{parent=Cosmology}
{c}
{wiki}

Luminosity distance is defined by $F=L/(4\pi d_L^2)$ and includes geometric dilution, photon redshift, and arrival-rate dilation.

= Cosmological flux dimming
{parent=Luminosity distance}
{c}

Expansion reduces bolometric flux by one factor of $1+z$ from photon energy and one from arrival rate, in addition to inverse-area dilution.

= Friedmann-Lemaitre-Robertson-Walker metric
{parent=Cosmology}
{c}
{wiki=Friedmann–Lemaître–Robertson–Walker_metric}

The FLRW metric is the homogeneous and isotropic spacetime metric with scale factor $a(t)$ and constant spatial curvature $k$.

= Spatial curvature of an FLRW universe
{parent=Friedmann-Lemaitre-Robertson-Walker metric}
{c}

The cases $k>0$, $k=0$, and $k<0$ give spherical, Euclidean, and hyperbolic constant-curvature spatial slices.

= Proper distance in cosmology
{parent=Friedmann-Lemaitre-Robertson-Walker metric}
{c}

Proper distance is the spatial metric length measured along a fixed-cosmic-time slice.

= Conformal time
{parent=Friedmann-Lemaitre-Robertson-Walker metric}
{c}
{wiki}

Conformal time satisfies $d\tau=dt/a(t)$ and factors the FLRW metric into $a^2(\tau)$ times a static conformal metric.

= Comoving radial distance
{parent=Conformal time}
{c}
{wiki=Comoving_and_proper_distances}

Comoving radial distance uses the coordinate $\chi=\int dr/\sqrt{1-kr^2}$ and removes the radial curvature factor.

= Radial null geodesic in FLRW spacetime
{parent=Conformal time}
{c}

In conformal coordinates a radial light ray obeys $d\chi/d\tau=\pm c$, so the cosmological light cone is at forty-five degrees after setting $c=1$.

= Cosmological continuity equation
{parent=Cosmology}
{c}
{wiki=Friedmann_equations#Fluid_equation}

Energy conservation in FLRW gives $\dot\rho=-3H(\rho+P)$ when $\rho$ and $P$ are energy density and pressure.

= Equation-of-state parameter
{parent=Cosmological continuity equation}
{c}
{wiki=Equation_of_state_(cosmology)}

A cosmological fluid with constant equation-of-state parameter $w$ has $P=w\rho$ and $\rho\propto a^{-3(1+w)}$.

= Quintessence
{parent=Equation-of-state parameter}
{c}
{wiki=Quintessence_(physics)}

Quintessence is dynamical dark energy with negative pressure, conventionally $-1<w<-1/3$.

= Quintessence scale-factor solution
{parent=Quintessence}
{c}

For $w=-2/3$, zero cosmological constant, and negative curvature, $a(t)=\sqrt\beta\,t+\gamma t^2/4$ with density and curvature parameters $\gamma,\beta>0$.

= Quintessence-dominated universe
{parent=Quintessence}
{c}

For $w=-2/3$, a quintessence-dominated scale factor grows quadratically and $tH$ approaches two.

= Age of an FLRW universe
{parent=Cosmology}
{c}
{wiki=Age_of_the_universe}

The age is the proper cosmic time elapsed from the scale-factor singularity $a=0$ to the present epoch.

= Hubble time
{parent=Age of an FLRW universe}
{c}
{wiki}

The Hubble time $H_0^{-1}$ is the expansion timescale inferred from the present Hubble parameter.

= Curvature-dominated universe
{parent=Cosmology}
{c}

In a curvature-dominated open FLRW regime, the scale factor is approximately linear in cosmic time and $tH$ approaches one.

= Deceleration parameter
{parent=Cosmology}
{c}
{wiki}

The deceleration parameter is $q=-a\ddot a/\dot a^2$; negative values describe accelerated expansion.

= Accelerating universe
{parent=Deceleration parameter}
{c}
{wiki=Accelerating_expansion_of_the_universe}

An accelerating universe has $\ddot a>0$, equivalently $q<0$ while expansion continues.

= Quantum theory
{parent=Mathematical physics}
{wiki=Quantum_mechanics}

= Quantum no-signalling
{parent=Quantum theory}
{wiki=No-communication_theorem}

An uncommunicated local trace-preserving operation cannot change the other subsystem's reduced state.

= Helstrom-Holevo bound
{c}
{parent=Quantum theory}
{wiki=Helstrom_measurement}

Equiprobable pure states of overlap magnitude $c$ have optimal discrimination probability $\frac12(1+\sqrt{1-c^2})$.

= Robertson uncertainty principle
{c}
{parent=Quantum theory}
{wiki=Robertson_uncertainty_relation}

$\Delta A\,\Delta B\ge\frac12|\langle[A,B]\rangle|$ in units $\hbar=1$.

= Coherent state
{parent=Quantum theory}
{wiki}

$|\alpha\rangle=D(\alpha)|0\rangle$ is normalized, satisfies $A|\alpha\rangle=\alpha|\alpha\rangle$, and evolves to a phase times $|\alpha e^{-i\omega t}\rangle$.

= Born approximation
{c}
{parent=Quantum theory}
{wiki}

First-order Lippmann-Schwinger iteration gives
$$
f(\mathbf k',\mathbf k)=-\frac{m}{2\pi\hbar^2}\widetilde V(\mathbf k'-\mathbf k).
$$

= Grover search algorithm
{c}
{parent=Quantum theory}
{wiki=Grover%27s_algorithm}

Grover search rotates a uniform state toward a marked subspace and finds one of $M$ marked entries using order $\sqrt{N/M}$ oracle calls.

= Deutsch-Jozsa algorithm
{c}
{parent=Quantum theory}
{wiki=Deutsch–Jozsa_algorithm}

The Deutsch-Jozsa algorithm uses phase kickback and interference to distinguish constant from perfectly balanced Boolean functions with one oracle call.

= Quantum Fourier transform
{parent=Quantum theory}
{wiki}

The quantum Fourier transform maps $|k\rangle$ to $N^{-1/2}\sum_j e^{2\pi ijk/N}|j\rangle$.

= Bell state
{c}
{parent=Quantum theory}
{wiki}

The four Bell states form an orthonormal maximally entangled basis for two qubits.

= Variational method
{parent=Quantum theory}
{wiki}

The Rayleigh quotient of any normalized trial state is at least the ground-state energy.

= Landau level
{c}
{parent=Quantum theory}
{wiki}

A charged particle in a uniform magnetic field has equally spaced orbital energy levels with guiding-centre degeneracy.

= Bloch theorem
{c}
{parent=Quantum theory}
{wiki=Bloch%27s_theorem}

In a periodic potential, energy eigenstates have the form $e^{ik\cdot x}u_k(x)$ with lattice-periodic $u_k$.

= Controlled unitary gate
{parent=Quantum theory}
{c}
{wiki=Controlled_NOT_gate#Controlled_U_gates}

A controlled-$U$ gate applies $U$ to its target exactly when its control qubit is one.

= Hadamard test
{parent=Controlled unitary gate}
{c}
{wiki}

The Hadamard test estimates the real part of $\langle\psi|U|\psi\rangle$ from one ancilla measurement.

= Imaginary-part Hadamard test
{parent=Hadamard test}
{c}

Adding a phase gate to the Hadamard test rotates the interference signal and measures the imaginary part of $\langle\psi|U|\psi\rangle$.

= Parallel quantum gates
{parent=Quantum theory}
{c}

Independent gates $A$ and $B$ on separate registers act jointly as $A\otimes B$.

= Measurement of a Pauli observable
{parent=Quantum theory}
{c}

The expectation of a Hermitian Pauli string can be obtained by basis rotation and measurement, or by a Hadamard test with the Pauli string as its controlled unitary.

= Statistical physics
{parent=Mathematical physics}
{wiki=Statistical_mechanics}

= Ideal gas free energy
{parent=Statistical physics}
{wiki=Ideal_gas}

Up to volume-independent terms, $F=-Nk_BT\log V$, so $-\partial F/\partial V=Nk_BT/V$.

= Electromagnetism
{parent=Mathematical physics}
{wiki}

= Relativistic Lorentz force
{c}
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki=Lorentz_force}

$m\,du^\mu/d\tau=qF^\mu{}_\nu u^\nu$ gives $d\mathbf p/dt=q(\mathbf E+\mathbf v\times\mathbf B)$ and $d(\gamma mc^2)/dt=q\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf v$.

= Charged particle in a uniform magnetic field
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{c}
{wiki}

A velocity cross magnetic-field force bends perpendicular motion into a circle while doing no work.

= Damped cyclotron motion
{parent=Charged particle in a uniform magnetic field}
{c}
{wiki}

Adding linear drag to cyclotron motion produces an exponentially contracting spiral.

= Electrostatics
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{c}
{wiki}

Electrostatics studies time-independent electric fields and charge distributions.

= Electrostatic energy
{parent=Electrostatics}
{c}
{wiki}

Electrostatic energy can be written as one half the charge-potential integral or epsilon-zero over two times the electric-field energy integral.

= Electric dipole moment
{parent=Electrostatics}
{c}
{wiki}

The electric dipole moment is the integral of position weighted by charge density.

= Maxwell equations
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{c}
{wiki}

Maxwell’s equations relate electric and magnetic fields to charge and current.

= Magnetic vector potential
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{c}
{wiki}

A magnetic vector potential satisfies B equal to its curl and is defined up to a gradient.

= Relativistic electromagnetism
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{c}
{wiki}

Relativistic electromagnetism combines electric and magnetic fields into Lorentz tensors.

= Electromagnetic field tensor
{parent=Relativistic electromagnetism}
{c}
{wiki}

The antisymmetric electromagnetic field tensor packages electric and magnetic components into a rank-two Lorentz tensor.

= Electromagnetic field invariants
{parent=Electromagnetic field tensor}
{c}
{wiki}

The two independent electromagnetic scalar invariants are determined by B squared minus E squared over c squared and by E dot B.

= Null electromagnetic field
{parent=Electromagnetic field invariants}
{c}
{wiki}

A null field has both electromagnetic invariants zero, as for perpendicular equal-magnitude radiation fields.

= Lorentz transformation of electromagnetic fields
{parent=Relativistic electromagnetism}
{c}
{wiki}

Lorentz boosts mix transverse electric and magnetic field components.

= Electromagnetic wave
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{c}
{wiki}

An electromagnetic wave is a propagating coupled oscillation of electric and magnetic fields governed by Maxwell's equations.

= Electromagnetic wave equation
{parent=Electromagnetic wave}
{c}
{wiki}

In vacuum, each field obeys $\nabla^2E-c^{-2}E_{tt}=0$ and $\nabla^2B-c^{-2}B_{tt}=0$.

= Plane electromagnetic wave
{parent=Electromagnetic wave}
{c}
{wiki}

For a vacuum plane wave, $k\cdot E_0=0$, $\omega=c|k|$, and $B_0=k\times E_0/\omega$.

= Poynting vector
{parent=Electromagnetic wave}
{c}
{wiki}

The Poynting vector $S=E\times B/\mu_0$ is electromagnetic energy flux.

= General relativity
{parent=Mathematical physics}
{wiki}

= Schwarzschild light deflection
{c}
{parent=General relativity}
{wiki=Gravitational_lens}

A null Schwarzschild geodesic with $b\gg r_s$ is deflected through $2r_s/b=4GM/(bc^2)$ to first order.

= Continuum mechanics
{parent=Mathematical physics}
{wiki}

= Boundary-layer scaling
{parent=Continuum mechanics}
{wiki=Boundary_layer}

Balancing $U^2/x$ with $\nu U/\delta^2$ gives $\delta\sim\sqrt{\nu x/U}$.

= Falkner-Skan equation
{c}
{parent=Continuum mechanics}
{wiki=Falkner–Skan_boundary_layer}

For $U=Ax^m$, $\psi=\sqrt{\nu xU}f(\eta)$ gives
$$
f'''+\frac{m+1}{2}ff''+m(1-f'^2)=0,
$$
with $f(0)=f'(0)=0$ and $f'(\infty)=1$.

= Acoustic energy flux
{parent=Continuum mechanics}
{wiki=Sound_intensity}

For harmonic acoustic amplitudes, $\langle\mathbf I\rangle=\frac12\operatorname{Re}(p\mathbf u^*)$.

= Burgers vortex
{c}
{parent=Continuum mechanics}
{wiki}

A Burgers vortex balances axial vortex stretching against radial viscous diffusion, producing a Gaussian vorticity profile.

= Added mass
{parent=Mathematical physics}
{c}
{wiki}

Added mass is the effective inertia contributed by fluid accelerated together with an immersed body.

= Biot-Savart law
{parent=Mathematical physics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Central force
{parent=Mathematical physics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Compton wavelength
{parent=Mathematical physics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Conservation of energy
{parent=Mathematical physics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Dimensional analysis
{parent=Mathematical physics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Effective potential
{parent=Mathematical physics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Electromagnetic boundary condition
{parent=Mathematical physics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Faraday law
{parent=Mathematical physics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Electromagnetic damping
{parent=Faraday law}
{c}
{wiki}

Motion-dependent induced current can produce a magnetic force opposing motion and dissipating energy resistively.

= Flux conservation in a superconducting loop
{parent=Faraday law}
{c}
{wiki}

An ideal superconducting loop supports persistent current that preserves linked magnetic flux when resistance vanishes.

= Fermat principle
{parent=Mathematical physics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Gauss law
{parent=Mathematical physics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Spherical shell theorem
{parent=Gauss law}
{c}
{wiki}

A uniformly charged spherical shell produces zero field inside and the field of a point charge outside.

= Hydrogen atom
{parent=Mathematical physics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Kepler third law
{parent=Mathematical physics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Magnetic monopole
{parent=Mathematical physics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Physical pendulum
{parent=Mathematical physics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Planck mass
{parent=Mathematical physics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Proper time
{parent=Mathematical physics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Quantum transmission
{parent=Mathematical physics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Relativistic aberration
{parent=Mathematical physics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Relativistic two-body decay
{parent=Mathematical physics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Rotating reference frame
{parent=Mathematical physics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Schrodinger equation
{parent=Mathematical physics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Schwarzschild radius
{parent=Mathematical physics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Shallow water equations
{parent=Mathematical physics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Stream function
{parent=Mathematical physics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Streamline
{parent=Mathematical physics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Vortex stretching
{parent=Mathematical physics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Vorticity equation
{parent=Mathematical physics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Beltrami flow
{parent=Vorticity equation}
{c}
{wiki}

A Beltrami flow has velocity parallel to vorticity.

= Special relativity
{parent=Mathematical physics}
{c}
{wiki}

Special relativity describes inertial frames using Lorentz transformations and Minkowski spacetime.

= Four-velocity
{parent=Special relativity}
{c}
{wiki}

Four-velocity is the proper-time derivative of position and has invariant squared norm c^2.

= Four-acceleration
{parent=Special relativity}
{c}
{wiki}

Four-acceleration is the proper-time derivative of four-velocity and is orthogonal to it.

= Proper acceleration
{parent=Four-acceleration}
{c}
{wiki}

Proper acceleration is the acceleration measured in the particle’s instantaneous rest frame.

= Rindler horizon
{parent=Proper acceleration}
{c}
{wiki}

A uniformly accelerated observer has a null boundary beyond which light signals can never reach the observer.

= Quantum mechanics
{parent=Mathematical physics}
{c}
{wiki}

Quantum mechanics models states by wavefunctions and observables by operators.

= Finite square well
{parent=Quantum mechanics}
{c}
{wiki}

A finite square well is a bounded interval on which the potential is lower than outside, supporting matched oscillatory and exponential states.

= Delta potential
{parent=Quantum mechanics}
{c}
{wiki}

A delta potential is a point interaction imposing continuity of the wavefunction and a jump in its derivative.

= Bound state in a delta potential
{parent=Delta potential}
{c}
{wiki}

An attractive one-dimensional delta potential has one even exponentially decaying bound state.

= Supersymmetric quantum mechanics
{parent=Quantum mechanics}
{c}
{wiki}

Supersymmetric quantum mechanics factors partner Hamiltonians through first-order operators.

= Partner Hamiltonians
{parent=Supersymmetric quantum mechanics}
{c}
{wiki}

Partner Hamiltonians reverse the order of two first-order factors and share corresponding positive-energy states.

= Reflectionless potential
{parent=Quantum mechanics}
{c}
{wiki}

A reflectionless potential transmits every scattering state with zero reflected probability.

= Position operator
{parent=Quantum mechanics}
{c}
{wiki}

In position representation, the position operator multiplies a wavefunction by its coordinate.

= Momentum operator
{parent=Quantum mechanics}
{c}
{wiki}

In position representation, momentum is minus i hbar times spatial differentiation.

= Ehrenfest theorem
{parent=Quantum mechanics}
{c}
{wiki}

Ehrenfest’s theorem makes expectation values obey Hamiltonian commutator equations analogous to classical motion.

= Angular momentum operator
{parent=Quantum mechanics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Infinite square well
{parent=Quantum mechanics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Particle in a rectangular box
{parent=Infinite square well}
{c}
{wiki=Particle_in_a_box}

Dirichlet separation in a rectangular box gives products of sine waves and energy proportional to $n_x^2/a^2+n_y^2/b^2+n_z^2/c^2$.

= Quantum degeneracy
{parent=Quantum mechanics}
{c}
{wiki=Degenerate_energy_levels}

An energy level is degenerate when its eigenspace has dimension greater than one.

= Orbital angular momentum
{parent=Quantum mechanics}
{c}
{wiki=Angular_momentum_operator}

Orbital angular momentum is $L=-i\hbar,x\times\nabla$; in particular $L_3=-i\hbar(x_1\partial_{x_2}-x_2\partial_{x_1})$.

= Angular momentum ladder variable
{parent=Orbital angular momentum}
{c}

The coordinate combinations $x_1\pm ix_2$ have $L_3$ eigenvalues $\pm\hbar$, so their powers carry magnetic quantum number $\pm m$.

= Three-dimensional isotropic harmonic oscillator
{parent=Quantum mechanics}
{c}
{wiki=Quantum_harmonic_oscillator#N-dimensional_isotropic_harmonic_oscillator}

Its energies are $E_N=\hbar\omega(N+3/2)$, and level $N$ has degeneracy $\binom{N+2}{2}$.

= Fluid mechanics
{parent=Mathematical physics}
{c}
{wiki}

Fluid mechanics studies the motion and forces of liquids and gases.

= Euler equations for an inviscid fluid
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{c}
{wiki}

Euler’s equations express momentum conservation in a fluid without viscosity.

= Bernoulli equation
{parent=Euler equations for an inviscid fluid}
{c}
{wiki}

For steady inviscid flow, pressure, kinetic energy density, and conservative potential are constant along a streamline.

= Bernoulli function
{parent=Bernoulli equation}
{c}
{wiki}

The Bernoulli function combines pressure, kinetic energy density, and conservative potential.

= Bernoulli equation over topography
{parent=Bernoulli equation}
{c}

For steady potential flow over small hills, linearized Bernoulli pressure combines a hydrostatic term $-\rho g\eta$ with a dynamic term $-\rho U\phi_x$.

= Integral momentum equation
{parent=Euler equations for an inviscid fluid}
{c}
{wiki}

The integral momentum equation balances momentum flux, pressure, body force, and forces on a control volume.

= Force on a pipe junction
{parent=Integral momentum equation}
{c}
{wiki}

The force on a pipe junction follows by balancing inlet and outlet momentum fluxes and pressure forces.

= Conservation of mass in a pipe
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{c}
{wiki}

For steady incompressible pipe flow, volume flux equals cross-sectional area times mean speed and is conserved through a junction.

= Viscous fluid flow
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{c}
{wiki}

Viscous fluid flow includes shear stresses proportional to velocity gradients for a Newtonian fluid.

= Falling film flow
{parent=Viscous fluid flow}
{c}
{wiki}

A steady film down a wall has a parabolic velocity profile set by gravity, ambient pressure gradient, and surface shear.

= Stress boundary condition
{parent=Viscous fluid flow}
{c}
{wiki}

At a fluid interface, normal and tangential tractions satisfy the imposed stress balance.

= No-slip boundary condition
{parent=Viscous fluid flow}
{c}
{wiki}

No slip requires fluid velocity to equal solid-boundary velocity.

= Hydrostatic pressure
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{c}
{wiki}

Hydrostatic pressure has gradient equal to fluid density times gravity.

= Potential flow
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{c}
{wiki}

Potential flow is irrotational flow represented as the gradient of a scalar potential.

= Squeezing wedge flow
{parent=Potential flow}
{c}
{wiki}

Inviscid flow between closing hinged plates is a quadratic potential flow that expels fluid radially.

= Streamfunction in polar coordinates
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{c}
{wiki}

For planar incompressible flow, a streamfunction satisfies u_r=psi_theta/r and u_theta=-psi_r.

= Incompressible flow
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Kinematic boundary condition
{parent=Fluid mechanics}
{c}
{wiki}

A material fluid boundary has no relative normal flow; at a fixed impermeable graph $y=\eta(x)$ this is $v=u\eta_x$.

= Linearized boundary condition
{parent=Kinematic boundary condition}
{c}

For a small boundary displacement and weak disturbance, evaluate boundary data on the undisturbed surface and discard products of small quantities.

= Conservation law
{parent=Mathematical physics}
{wiki}

A conservation law equates local time change of a density with the divergence of its flux.

= Continuity equation
{parent=Conservation law}
{c}
{wiki}

The continuity equation expresses local conservation as time change plus flux divergence equal to zero.

= Geometry and topology
{parent=Mathematics}
{wiki}

= Algebraic topology
{parent=Geometry and topology}
{wiki}

= Lefschetz number
{c}
{parent=Algebraic topology}
{wiki}

$L(f)=\sum_i(-1)^i\operatorname{tr}(f_*:H_i\to H_i)$, equivalently the alternating trace on simplicial chains.

= Lefschetz fixed-point theorem
{c}
{parent=Algebraic topology}
{wiki}

If $L(f)\ne0$, then $f$ has a fixed point. For a fixed-point-free map, sufficiently fine simplicial approximation has zero diagonal chain coefficients, hence zero Lefschetz number.

= Mayer-Vietoris theorem
{c}
{parent=Algebraic topology}
{wiki=Mayer–Vietoris_sequence}

Mayer-Vietoris gives a long exact homology sequence for a space decomposed into two suitable subspaces.

= Simplicial cone
{parent=Algebraic topology}
{c}
{wiki=Cone_(topology)}

The simplicial cone $v*K$ adjoins a new vertex $v$ to every simplex of $K$ and is contractible.

= Suspension of a topological space
{parent=Algebraic topology}
{c}
{wiki=Suspension_(topology)}

The suspension is the union of two cones on a space along their common base.

= Reduced homology of a suspension
{parent=Suspension of a topological space}
{c}

Suspension shifts reduced homology: $\widetilde H_n(\Sigma X)\cong\widetilde H_{n-1}(X)$.

= Union of cones along a common base
{parent=Suspension of a topological space}
{c}

The union of $m$ cones on a nonempty space along one common base is homotopy equivalent to a wedge of $m-1$ suspensions of that space.

= Algebraic geometry
{parent=Geometry and topology}
{wiki}

= Birational variety
{parent=Algebraic geometry}
{wiki=Birational_geometry}

Irreducible varieties are birational exactly when they have isomorphic function fields.

= Normalization of a nodal curve
{parent=Algebraic geometry}
{wiki=Normalization_(algebraic_geometry)}

Normalization separates a node's two branches; a rational nodal cubic has normalization $\mathbb P^1$ and two points above its node.

= Riemann-Roch theorem
{c}
{parent=Algebraic geometry}
{wiki=Riemann–Roch_theorem}

$\ell(D)-\ell(K-D)=\deg D+1-g$ relates meromorphic functions with prescribed poles to the geometry of a curve.

= Rational map of projective varieties
{parent=Algebraic geometry}
{c}
{wiki=Rational_mapping}

A rational map is a morphism on a dense open subset, considered up to agreement on a smaller dense open subset.

= Indeterminacy locus
{parent=Rational map of projective varieties}
{c}
{wiki=Indeterminacy_locus}

The indeterminacy locus is the set where a rational map has no regular local representative.

= Projective Cremona transformation
{parent=Rational map of projective varieties}
{c}
{wiki=Cremona_transformation}

A projective Cremona transformation is a birational self-map of projective space, regular on complementary dense open subsets together with its inverse.

= Nonsingular plane cubic
{parent=Algebraic geometry}
{c}
{wiki=Elliptic_curve}

A plane cubic is nonsingular when its homogeneous equation and all first partial derivatives have no common projective zero.

= Differential geometry
{parent=Geometry and topology}
{wiki}

= Curvature and torsion
{parent=Differential geometry}
{wiki=Curvature}

For unit speed, $\kappa=|T'|$ and $\tau=-B'\cdot N$. Generally $\kappa=|r'\times r''|/|r'|^3$ and $\tau=\det(r',r'',r''')/|r'\times r''|^2$.

= Geodesic curvature
{parent=Differential geometry}
{wiki}

For a surface curve, $T'=\kappa_n n+\kappa_g(n\times T)$. It is geodesic exactly when $\kappa_g=0$.

= Gauss-Bonnet theorem
{c}
{parent=Differential geometry}
{wiki=Gauss–Bonnet_theorem}

$$
\int_DK\,dA+\int_{\partial D}k_g\,ds+\sum\text{ exterior angles}=2\pi\chi(D).
$$

= Gaussian curvature of a torus
{parent=Gauss-Bonnet theorem}
{c}
{wiki}

For a standard ring torus, Gaussian curvature is positive outside, negative inside, and zero along the top and bottom circles.

= Total Gaussian curvature
{parent=Gauss-Bonnet theorem}
{c}
{wiki}

The total Gaussian curvature of a closed surface is two pi times its Euler characteristic.

= Preimage theorem
{c}
{parent=Differential geometry}
{wiki}

The inverse image of a regular value of a smooth map is a submanifold of codimension equal to the target dimension.

= Theorema Egregium
{c}
{parent=Differential geometry}
{wiki}

Gaussian curvature depends only on the first fundamental form and is therefore preserved by local isometries.

= Riemannian geometry
{parent=Differential geometry}
{wiki}

Riemannian geometry studies smooth manifolds equipped with smoothly varying inner products on tangent spaces.

= Energy of a curve
{parent=Riemannian geometry}
{c}

The energy of a curve is one half the integral of its squared speed.

= Christoffel symbol
{parent=Riemannian geometry}
{c}
{wiki}

Christoffel symbols are the coordinate coefficients of the Levi-Civita connection.

= Geodesic equation
{parent=Riemannian geometry}
{c}
{wiki}

A geodesic has zero covariant acceleration and in coordinates satisfies $\ddot u^k+\Gamma^k_{ij}\dot u^i\dot u^j=0$.

= Normal section of a surface
{parent=Geodesic equation}
{c}

A constant-speed intersection with a plane containing every surface normal along the curve is a geodesic.

= Embedded surface parametrization
{parent=Differential geometry}
{c}
{wiki}

An embedded-surface chart is a homeomorphic smooth parametrisation with derivative of rank two.

= Surface of revolution
{parent=Differential geometry}
{c}
{wiki}

A surface of revolution is invariant under rotations around a fixed axis and is locally generated by rotating a profile curve.

= Ruled surface
{parent=Differential geometry}
{c}
{wiki}

A ruled surface is swept out by a smoothly varying one-parameter family of affine lines.

= One-sheet hyperboloid
{parent=Ruled surface}
{c}
{wiki}

A one-sheet hyperboloid is a connected doubly ruled quadric, diffeomorphic to a cylinder.

= Flat cone
{parent=Differential geometry}
{c}
{wiki}

A cone is intrinsically flat away from its vertex but has nontrivial angular holonomy.

= Developing map
{parent=Flat cone}
{c}
{wiki}

A developing map locally unfolds a flat surface into the Euclidean plane.

= Isometry of a cone
{parent=Flat cone}
{c}
{wiki}

Cone isometries preserve its angular identification; those fixing a point are the identity or an axial-plane reflection.

= Manifold chart
{parent=Differential geometry}
{c}
{wiki=Manifold#Charts}

A chart is a homeomorphism from an open subset of a manifold to an open subset of Euclidean space.

= No compact manifold has a single Euclidean chart
{parent=Manifold chart}
{c}

A nonempty compact manifold cannot be homeomorphic to an open subset of Euclidean space, since no nonempty Euclidean open set is compact.

= Regular value
{parent=Differential geometry}
{c}
{wiki=Regular_value}

A value is regular when the derivative is surjective at every point of its preimage.

= Symplectic group
{parent=Differential geometry}
{c}
{wiki}

The real symplectic group consists of matrices $A$ satisfying $A^TJA=J$ and has dimension $2n^2+n$ in size $2n$.

= Tangent space of the symplectic group
{parent=Symplectic group}
{c}

At the identity, the symplectic Lie algebra consists of $X$ satisfying $X^TJ+JX=0$; tangent spaces elsewhere are its left translates.

= Grassmannian as projection matrices
{parent=Differential geometry}
{c}
{wiki=Grassmannian}

The real Grassmannian of $k$-planes is represented by symmetric idempotent matrices of trace $k$.

= Rank-one orthogonal projection
{parent=Grassmannian as projection matrices}
{c}

Every rank-one orthogonal projection has the form $xx^T$ for a unit vector, uniquely up to replacing $x$ by $-x$.

= Real projective plane
{parent=Grassmannian as projection matrices}
{c}
{wiki}

The real projective plane is the quotient of $S^2$ by $x\sim-x$ and parametrizes lines through the origin in $\mathbb R^3$.

= Classification theorem for surfaces
{parent=Geometry and topology}
{c}
{wiki}

= Conformal map
{parent=Geometry and topology}
{c}
{wiki}

= Connected space
{parent=Geometry and topology}
{c}
{wiki}

= Continuous image of a connected space
{parent=Connected space}
{c}
{wiki}

A continuous image of a connected space is connected.

= Closure of a connected set
{parent=Connected space}
{c}
{wiki}

Every set lying between a connected set and its closure is connected.

= Distance from a point to a plane
{parent=Geometry and topology}
{c}
{wiki}

= Elliptic coordinates
{parent=Geometry and topology}
{c}
{wiki}

= Joukowski map
{parent=Geometry and topology}
{c}
{wiki}

= Orientation of a surface
{parent=Geometry and topology}
{c}
{wiki}

= Path-connected space
{parent=Geometry and topology}
{c}
{wiki}

= Plane
{parent=Geometry and topology}
{c}
{wiki}

= Plane Poiseuille flow
{parent=Geometry and topology}
{c}
{wiki}

= Product topology
{parent=Geometry and topology}
{c}
{wiki}

= Quotient topology
{parent=Geometry and topology}
{c}
{wiki}

= Sequentially compact space
{parent=Geometry and topology}
{c}
{wiki}

= Sphere-plane intersection
{parent=Geometry and topology}
{c}
{wiki}

= Spherically symmetric solution of Poisson equation
{parent=Geometry and topology}
{c}
{wiki}

= Topological degree
{parent=Geometry and topology}
{c}
{wiki}

Topological degree is an integer-valued signed count of preimages that is stable under suitable perturbations.

= Trigonometric function
{parent=Geometry and topology}
{c}
{wiki}

= Euclidean geometry
{parent=Geometry and topology}
{wiki}

Euclidean geometry studies distances, angles, and rigid figures in flat space.

= Thales theorem
{parent=Euclidean geometry}
{c}
{wiki=Thales%27s_theorem}

An angle subtended by a diameter of a circle is right; conversely, the hypotenuse of a right triangle inscribed in a circle is a diameter.

= Regular polygon
{parent=Euclidean geometry}
{c}
{wiki}

A regular polygon has equal side lengths and equal interior angles. Its vertices on a circle are scaled and rotated roots of unity.

= Area of a regular octagon
{parent=Regular polygon}
{c}
{wiki}

A regular octagon of side length s has area 2(1+sqrt(2))s^2.

= Spherical cap
{parent=Euclidean geometry}
{c}
{wiki}

A spherical cap is the part of a sphere cut off by a plane.

= Lattice polygon
{parent=Euclidean geometry}
{c}
{wiki}

A lattice polygon has all vertices in the integer lattice; triangulation shows that its area is a half-integer.

= Spherical symmetry
{parent=Geometry and topology}
{c}
{wiki}

Spherical symmetry means invariance under every orthogonal transformation fixing the centre.

= Isotropic tensor integral
{parent=Spherical symmetry}
{c}
{wiki}

A rotationally invariant rank-two tensor is a scalar multiple of $\delta_{ij}$; taking its trace determines the scalar.

= Differential form
{parent=Geometry and topology}
{wiki}

A differential form is an alternating covariant tensor field designed for coordinate-independent integration.

= Exact differential
{parent=Differential form}
{c}
{wiki}

An exact differential is the differential $df$ of a scalar potential and integrates to zero around every closed curve.

= Topology
{parent=Geometry and topology}
{c}
{wiki}

Topology studies spaces and properties preserved by continuous deformation.

= Hausdorff space
{parent=Topology}
{c}
{wiki}

A Hausdorff space separates any two distinct points by disjoint open neighborhoods.

= Closed diagonal theorem
{parent=Hausdorff space}
{c}
{wiki}

A space is Hausdorff exactly when its diagonal is closed in its square.

= Closed graph theorem for compact spaces
{parent=Topology}
{c}
{wiki}

A map between compact Hausdorff spaces is continuous exactly when its graph is closed.

= Topological surface
{parent=Topology}
{c}
{wiki}

A topological surface is a Hausdorff second-countable space locally homeomorphic to the plane.

= Polygonal schema
{parent=Topological surface}
{c}
{wiki}

A polygonal schema constructs a surface by identifying paired polygon edges.

= Torus
{parent=Topological surface}
{c}
{wiki}

The torus is the product of two circles and can be formed by identifying opposite sides of a square.

= Double cone singularity
{parent=Topological surface}
{c}
{wiki}

The vertex of a double cone is not a surface point because its punctured neighborhood disconnects into two pieces.

= Euler characteristic
{parent=Topological surface}
{c}
{wiki}

Euler characteristic is V-E+F for a finite cell structure and is independent of the chosen structure.

= Hyperbolic geometry
{parent=Geometry and topology}
{c}
{wiki}

Hyperbolic geometry studies spaces of constant negative curvature.

= Hyperbolic plane
{parent=Hyperbolic geometry}
{c}
{wiki}

The hyperbolic plane is the simply connected two-dimensional geometry of constant curvature minus one.

= Finite-order elliptic transformation
{parent=Hyperbolic plane}
{c}
{wiki}

A noncentral finite-order orientation-preserving hyperbolic isometry fixes an interior point and is elliptic.

= Poincare disc model
{parent=Hyperbolic geometry}
{c}
{wiki=Poincaré_disk_model}

The Poincare disc carries the metric $4|dz|^2/(1-|z|^2)^2$ on $|z|<1$.

= Poincare half-plane model
{parent=Hyperbolic geometry}
{c}
{wiki=Poincaré_half-plane_model}

The upper half-plane carries the hyperbolic metric $(dx^2+dy^2)/y^2$.

= Cayley transform
{parent=Hyperbolic geometry}
{c}
{wiki}

The map $z\mapsto i(1+z)/(1-z)$ is an isometry from the Poincare disc to the upper half-plane.

= Hyperbolic triangle
{parent=Hyperbolic geometry}
{c}
{wiki}

A hyperbolic triangle is bounded by three hyperbolic geodesics and may have vertices on the ideal boundary.

= Ideal vertex
{parent=Hyperbolic triangle}
{c}
{wiki=Ideal_triangle}

An ideal vertex lies on the boundary at infinity and has internal angle zero.

= Hyperbolic triangle area
{parent=Hyperbolic triangle}
{c}

For curvature $-1$, a triangle of angles $\alpha,\beta,\gamma$ has area $\pi-\alpha-\beta-\gamma$ by Gauss--Bonnet.

= Hyperbolic law of cosines
{parent=Hyperbolic triangle}
{c}
{wiki=Hyperbolic_law_of_cosines}

For opposite side $a$, $\cosh a=(\cos\alpha+\cos\beta\cos\gamma)/(\sin\beta\sin\gamma)$.

= Adjoint operator
{parent=Mathematics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Basel problem
{parent=Mathematics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Cantor function
{parent=Mathematics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Cayley-Hamilton theorem
{parent=Mathematics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Chinese remainder theorem
{parent=Mathematics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Classification of isolated singularities
{parent=Mathematics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Essential singularity
{parent=Classification of isolated singularities}
{c}
{wiki}

An isolated singularity is essential when its Laurent series has infinitely many nonzero negative-power terms.

= Conjugacy class
{parent=Mathematics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Cycle decomposition of a permutation
{parent=Mathematics}
{c}
{wiki}

= Euler criterion
